\par
Historical Context of The Grapes of Wrath \par
\par
 \par
\par
Dr Sarah Robertson, University of West England \par
\par
 \par
\par
Steinbeck's novel was published in 1939 and is set during the 1930s, which was one of the most turbulent decades in 20th century American history. It was turbulent, in part because this was the decade known as the Great Depression and the Great Depression had been set into motion by the economic downturn at the end of the 1920s, which was marked by the Wall Street crash on the 24th of October 1929. And the Wall Street crash sent the nation into a series of economic crises and panic and despair. And that depression would last for almost a decade until 1939 and the beginning of the Second World War, when the American economy began to improve as American industries began to produce supplies for allied forces and then eventually for the American war effort when the United States entered the war in 1941. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So when we read and consider Steinbeck's novel, we have that broader sense of the Great Depression in mind. But more specifically to the Joad family and their experience in this novel, and indeed the other migrants that Steinbeck is interested in. We also have to consider the notion of the Dust Bowl, which affected Oklahoma, one of the states in the novel, the Home State of the Joad family and the Dust Bowl affected five main states in the area known as the Great Plains. The Dust Bowl was created in part by a severe drought throughout the 1930s. \par
\par
 \par
\par
But Steinbeck is interested in this novel and taking us beyond just here and now of the 1930s and the severe drought to understand a number of the agricultural and economic issues that also led to that drought in the 1930s. And he does this by referencing Native Americans and the relationship between the settler families, such as the jobs and the native peoples who came before them. So, for example, in Inter-chapter five, we have this exchange between a landowner and tenant. So the landowner says:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
You'll have to get off the land. The ploughs will go through the door yard, and now the squatting men stood up angrily. Grandpa took up the land, and he had to kill the Indians and drive them away, and Pa was born here, and he killed weeds and snakes. (35) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Steinbeck is pointing us here to the fact that when the settlers first arrived in the Great Plains in the territories west of the Mississippi, River that the land itself was still being fought over by the federal government and Native American peoples. By drawing our attention to the plight of native peoples and their forced removal from the land. Steinbeck helps us to understand that as the jobs are forced off the land in the 1930s, no one ever truly owns this land. \par
\par
 \par
\par
That land is shaped by economic imperatives and will never belong to any one person, but more to the economic structures of the given time. And what we see in the Great Plains with the settlers is that they have to make that area economically viable. And to do so, they have to plant numerous crops, including cotton. And to do that, they had to clear the prairies of prairie grasses, which had helped to maintain the biodiversity of the Great Plains. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So when the severe drought happens in the 1930s, there's no protection for the land with the prairie grass has gone, so Steinbeck is interested not just in the fact that there's a severe drought. But the fact that poor land management and the need to make land profitable also contributed to the Dust Bowl situation of the 1930s. So, with the Great Depression in the dust Bowl in mind, it's clear then that thousands of people during the depression were suffering from economic hardships, and the plight of those people came to national attention, perhaps more than any other way through photography. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So a number of famous photographers of the time Dorothea Lange, Margaret Bourke White and Walker Evans went to the south and westward to capture the plight of the nation's poor. And Steinbeck would have been familiar with these images, including Dorothy Lange's famous migrant mother, which she took in 1936 and which has become the image most associated with that period and particularly the specific plight of migrants. But for Steinbeck, he wanted to go beyond just the images and the news stories that he was seeing and reading at the time. So in 1936 Steinbeck produced a series of stories about a squatter camp that he had visited in California, and a year later, in 1937 he actually travelled with a group of migrants from Oklahoma to California and all of those insights that he gained with the migrants he used to depict the Joad family and the wider migrant experience in his novel, which he published in 1939. \par
\par
