 \par
\par
For many, Lange\rquote s Migrant Mother, Nipomo, California is the single most recognizable image from the Great Depression, epitomizing the desperate circumstances many found themselves in during that period. The now-iconic photograph was made for the US government\rquote s Resettlement Administration (renamed the Farm Security Administration, or FSA, in 1937), a federal agency created to document and remedy the plight of the urban and rural poor in the 1930s. The photograph\rquote s pictorial strength and emotional impact, combined with its recurring presence in newspapers, magazines, exhibitions, and displays, cemented its place in America\rquote s collective memory of the era. \par
\par
In the image, thirty-two-year-old migrant farmworker Florence Owens Thompson and three of her children are depicted huddled together in a tent at a pea-pickers\rquote  camp in Nipomo, California. Lange\rquote s taut composition excludes all but the most essential information. In lieu of the girls\rquote  faces, we see their tousled heads nestling against their mother\rquote s shoulders; their anonymity serves to lend these familial bonds a sense of universality. Still, Lange\rquote s large negative captures a wealth of detail that anchors our experience in specific fact, from the frayed fabric to Thompson\rquote s weary, concerned, strikingly beautiful face. \par
\par
Lange\rquote s work connected the descriptive style of documentary photography with the principle of social engagement. It has become a touchstone for photographers who feel that their work should not only record social conditions but also persuade people to improve them. \par
