 \par
\par
The term The American Dream was only coined in 1931 by James Truslow Adams, which is much later than many people think. And since it came into popular usage, the term has come become synonymous with notions of material success and financial wealth to things that Steinbeck is writing against in The Grapes of Wrath. What Steinbeck is interested in in what he's championing instead is small scale, independent farming and we can read his interest in these things in terms of a wider set and a broader set of American thinking that goes beyond the limits of the American dream. And we're best able to explore these issues by turning to the inter-chapters of the novel, where Steinbeck has more space and time to reflect on these ideas outside of his depiction of the Joad family. So this is a short extract from inter-chapter 11, where Steinbeck offers a detailed explanation of what type of farming and attachment to the land he privileges and celebrates. So he writes: \par
\par
 \par
\par
And when a horse stops work and goes into the barn there is a life and vitality left, there is a breathing and a warmth [\'85] There is a warmth of life in the barn, and the heat and smell of life. But when the motor of a tractor stops, it is as dead as the ore it came from. The heat goes out of it like the living heat that leaves the corpse [\'85]So easy that the wonder goes out of work so efficient that the wonder goes out of land and working of it, and with the wonder, the deep understanding and the relation. (120) \par
\par
 \par
\par
So here Steinbeck is critically interrogating industrial approaches to farming. He repeats the words warmth and wonder as he describes traditional farming practises, whereas the machinery of modernity the tractor is presented as death-like and corpse-like so here we see Steinbeck challenging a form of small, independent farming that can be read in terms of agrarianism and agrarianism, has a long tradition in American thinking. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And to go back to the earliest forms of agrarian thinking, we have to turn to the founding father and writer of the Declaration of Independence, Thomas Jefferson, who was also the third president of the nation. Jefferson was a champion of democracy. He was anti-monarchy and anti-aristocracy, and he believed in the individual rights of man. Tied to these notions was a celebration of agrarianism and traditional farming practises. \par
\par
 \par
\par
He believed that true value rested in man's attachment to the land because in short, agrarianism is a social or political philosophy that champions the rural over the urban and believes that humanity's integrity comes from its connection to the land. So, in 1785 Jefferson wrote Notes on the State of Virginia in which he argued \ldblquote Those who labour in the Earth are the chosen people of God.\rdblquote  And of course, Jefferson's observations are problematic because, like many founding fathers, he was a slave owner and, for slave owners, the slaves who were toiling the Earth were not God's chosen people but nevertheless, Jefferson's notion that labouring on the earth had integrity and is an honourable profession is a strain that runs through Steinbeck's novel. Take, for example, Connie, who for all intents and purposes, is an anti-agrarian. Connie wants a life in an urban environment. He has no desire to be a farmer. He seeks out material acquisitions and, in order to achieve those, he in a very cowardly manner abandons his pregnant wife and family. So, this anti-agrarian Connie is presented as without integrity, without honour and honourability and it's only those who have either a deep connection to the land or to the idea of family who have presented as honourable in Steinbeck's novel. Despite Steinbeck's championing of small-scale independent farming, he is more than aware that by the time of the 1930s and period in which he's writing the novel that those notions are merely idealistic. Because Steinbeck is interesting in depicting the changes that are taking place as small-scale farming is being replaced by big-scale, big-business farming. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The sense of change that he depicts comes through in inter-chapter five, where we see the exchanges between a tractor driver and tenants (39) And it becomes clear through those exchanges that with the advent of modernity in the 1930s, farming is now big business, that to be a successful farmer, the farmer has to be able to purchase vast acres of land and the machinery with which to farm it. So, Steinbeck is depicting what is commonly referred to now as a move to agri-business. The term agri-business only comes into effect in 1957, so almost 20 years after Steinbeck was writing The Grapes of Wrath, so it was not a term within his vocabulary. But the transition that he's depicting between small independent farming and big scale big business farming is that move to agri-business, and he's interested not just in the fact that the change itself is happening, but how that change is actually affecting humanity and society's understanding of economic structures. So, in inter Chapter 19, for example, Steinbeck goes into great detail about the changes not just to the land, but how that changes how humans interact with one another (243). He depicts how land has become a commodity, how it has become a scientific process. He writes about chemists testing the soil and that under this climate, farmers simply become storekeepers. Steinbeck doesn't outrightly demonise the landowners who take over from families like the Joads. He does try to offer a nuanced portrait of the pressures on landowners as well as the tenants but where he does critique landowners is where they failed to take responsibility for the changes that they are setting into effect. In inter-chapter five (35), for example, he talks at length about landowner men and how they talk about this change in farming practises, and he imagines a conversation between these landowners and tenants. And he writes: \par
\par
 \par
\par
We\rquote re sorry. It\rquote s not us. It\rquote s the monster. The bank isn\rquote t like a man. \par
\par
Yes, but the bank is only made of men. \par
\par
No, you\rquote re wrong there\emdash quite wrong there. The bank is something else than men. It happens that every man in a bank hates what the bank does, and yet the bank does it. The bank is something more than men, I tell you. It\rquote s the monster. Men made it, but they can\rquote t control it. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Part of Steinbeck\rquote s approach in this novel is to challenge that perspective. He wants his readers to understand that the bank isn't a monster, the bank is a human made entity and that we have to understand that if we're at all to resist and challenge the status quo, so where he critiques the landowners in this move to big business, he's doing it in a way that sets up a platform for questioning the ability to resist. \par
\par
 \par
