\par
Hope and Despair \par
\par
 \par
\par
Dr Sarah Robertson, University of West England \par
\par
 \par
\par
So in this lecture, I'm going to talk about the notions of hope and despair as they play out in Steinbeck's The Grapes of Wrath. It's clear from the beginning of this novel that despair is everywhere. We encounter at the beginning the isolated, lonely figure of Mulvey Graves, whose very name connotes the fact that he is connected with impending death and doom because of his inability to shift and change. We encounter the deaths of the grandparents on the road to California and there's so much despair when the families reach California to find so much unused land when the migrants only want a small parcel for themselves to feed their families. \par
\par
 \par
\par
We also have the death of Rose of Sharon's baby and the death of Casey the preacher. Despair is also acutely found in Steinbeck's novel as he interrogates and critiques long held American beliefs. So in inter-chapter nine (91), for example, he interrogates the idea that Americans can start again: \par
\par
 \par
\par
Maybe we can start again, in the new rich land\emdash in California, where the fruit grows. We\rquote ll start over. \par
\par
But you can\rquote t start. Only a baby can start. You and me\emdash why, we\rquote re all that\rquote s been. The anger of a moment, the thousand pictures, that\rquote s us. This land, this red land, is us; and the flood years and the dust years and the drought years are us. We can\rquote t start again. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So Steinbeck is critiquing that very American notion of being able to start again, which you might be able to compare to F. Scott Fitzgerald's The Great Gatsby, where no matter how much he's tried, Gatsby can never really lose being James Gatz, so that inability to start again as a direct critique of fundamental American thinking. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Similarly, in inter-chapter 12 (125), you might consider the critique Steinbeck makes of the notion of American freedom. So as Steinbeck depicts the migrant experience, he shows that long held traditional ideas of freedom in America simply no longer hold in a very capitalist, economically driven environment.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
There is so much despair in this novel, but hope is also a strong feature of Steinbeck's writing. Indeed, the novel moves towards a very optimistic end. Some might argue, too optimistic, but nevertheless, that's where Steinbeck takes us as readers and he does that through an interrogation and understanding if the notion of cooperation of working together, which is something that Casey thinks about in Chapter 16. Casey says:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
They\rquote s gonna come somepin outa all these folks goin\rquote  wes\rquote\emdash  outa all their farms lef\rquote  lonely. They\rquote s gonna come a thing that\rquote s gonna change the whole country.\rdblquote  (181) \par
\par
 \par
\par
In Casey\rquote s understanding, what's going to change the country is a sense of communalism and cooperation, and I'm going to talk more about both of those things in the final lecture.  \par
\par
As we think about hope and despair in Steinbeck's novel, it's useful to compare the two main camps that the Joads experience in California, the Hooverville camp and then the Weed Patch camp. The first camp they encounter is the Hooverville Camp, and Hooverville camps were named after the president in place at the start of the Great Depression, Herbert Hoover, and Hoover was a Republican, and he strongly believed that it was not the role of government to intervene to help the plight of poor people. He believed that it was the individual responsibility of people or families to improve their own situation. Because of that outlook, and because so many migrant camps appeared around the country, not just in California, we see those camps and their squalid living conditions being named after Hoover \endash  Hoovervilles \endash  and were introduced to the Hooverville in Chapter 20 of the novel, and immediately we're told there was no order in the camp and throughout Steinbeck's description of the Hooverville, he uses words such as \lquote scattered\rquote  \lquote rusty\rquote , \lquote mouldy\rquote  and \lquote tattered\rquote  to underline that this is a place of despair and desperation. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Hoover's policies were so unpopular that when the presidential election came around in 1932, he was unable to win against the Democratic candidate, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, who had a landslide victory based on his idea of a New Deal for the Nation. And his New Deal policies were designed to help the poor because Roosevelt believed it was the government's responsibility to intervene in moments of Great Depression and one of the things that emerges out of Roosevelt's New Deal programmes is something called the New Deal Farm Labour Camps, which the Weed Patch camp in the novel is based on. So Steinbeck's Weed Patch camp is specifically based on the Arvin Federal Government Camp in Kern County, California, and these New Deal camps were designed not just to improve the living conditions of migrants, but also to encourage and foster cooperation amongst migrants so, for example, that they might be able to work together to improve their workers' rights. Now as Steinbeck depicts the Weed Patch camp in the novel he does idealise these New Deal camps. In reality, New Deal camps were filled with strife. There wasn't the sense of cooperation that the people who founded them had hoped for, but they did improve living conditions, and that is something that Steinbeck accurately depicts in the novel. So, when in Chapter 22 the Joads first arrive at the Weed Patch Camp, here we're told that, unlike, the unruly Hooverville, that the rows were straight and there was no litter about the tents. Where Steinbeck most idealises the Weed Patch camp is in the notion of self-government and self-organisation. In Steinbeck's novel, the people rule themselves with committees and elections, whereas in fact, in the New Deal camps, there was a mixture of federal agency oversight and people working together. But Steinbeck's interest in people working together is what comes out of that Weed Patch account. Steinbeck was clearly writing in a period where there was still some hope for socio-political change, and perhaps that's why he ends the novel on such an optimistic note. Critics, detractors of the novel are concerned about the overly optimistic end of the novel, particularly as Rose of Sharon breast feeds the dying man. But more broadly in that final chapter, Steinbeck is championing a sense of cooperation so we can think of Rose of Sharon, but we might also think about Ruthie, who is collecting rose petals flower petals along the way, and she gradually understands that she has to share with her brother. And it's those smaller moments of shifting thought processes that Steinbeck is trying to capture at the end. It's the small moments that can bring about change. Steinbeck was hoping that communalism would win out over individualism. \par
\par
 \par
\par
That's not quite where society went after he wrote the novel, and in the next lecture, I'm going to talk more about his interest in how we might move from individualism to communalism. But before I do that, we get a sense of Steinbeck's philosophy through Casey. So this is Chapter 18, where Casey observes: \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote If he needs a million acres to make him feel rich, seems to me he needs it \rquote cause he feels awful poor inside hisself, and if he\rquote s poor in hisself, there ain\rquote t no million acres gonna make him feel rich, an\rquote  maybe he\rquote s disappointed that nothin\rquote  he can do\rquote ll make him feel rich\emdash not rich like Mis\rquote  Wilson was when she give her tent when Grampa died. (216) \par
\par
 \par
\par
So here we see a sense that hope in this novel is filtered not through material gain. \par
\par
 \par
\par
