\par
From \ldblquote I\rdblquote  to \ldblquote We\rdblquote  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Dr Sarah Robertson, University of West England \par
\par
 \par
\par
 \par
\par
So in this final lecture, I'm going to discuss the notion of journeys in the novel, particularly in relation to the journey that Steinbeck hopes that his readers will take alongside Tom Joad and Ma from a notion of \ldblquote I\rdblquote  to an understanding of \ldblquote we.\rdblquote  Journeys are central to The Grapes of Wrath \endash  no more so than the fact that the Joad family make migratory journey from Oklahoma to California, but there are also a number of metaphorical, symbolic journeys in the course of the novel. You may have thought about the turtle that Tom finds at the beginning of the text and then sets free, and we also have Ma Joad\rquote s journey to becoming the head of the family, which raises all manner of questions around gender politics. \par
\par
 \par
\par
But I wanted to set this discussion more broadly within the American context of journeys and, in American thinking, the journey, and even more particularly the road journey, is aligned with notions of escape, of freedom, of man's ability to assert his independence outside of society, and that's connected to a fundamental American belief in the notion of starting again and regeneration, ideas that I said in the previous lecture. Steinbeck is critiquing, but he shows us the hope that the Joads have in that notion of the American journey is they set out to California based on nothing more than the marketing material that they've been given. \par
\par
 \par
\par
But what marks the Joads\rquote  journey is very different from the traditional American journey is that they are migrants. There's isn\rquote t a journey undertaken to achieve self fulfilment. It's not an existential journey to understand the self. The Joads are migrants. They are forced off the land and its that process, that state of migration and being a migrant, that Steinbeck hopes that we understandand perhaps what makes it a very relevant novel today. Steinbeck, interestingly, takes Route 66 which is the epitome of the American road journey, the road that\rquote s celebrated with those notions of freedom and escape and he places over that the notion of migration. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So his detailed account of Route 66 comes in inter-chapter 12, where he writes that:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Highway 66 is the main migrant road. 66\emdash the long concrete path across the country, waving gently up and down on the map, from the Mississippi to Bakersfield\emdash over the red lands and the gray lands, twisting up into the mountains, crossing the Divide and down into the bright and terrible desert, and across the desert to the mountains again, and into the rich California valleys. \par
\par
66 is the path of a people in flight, refugees from dust and shrinking land, from the thunder of tractors and shrinking ownership, from the desert\rquote s slow northward invasion, from the twisting winds that howl up out of Texas, from the floods that bring no richness to the land and steal what little richness is there. (123) \par
\par
 \par
\par
and throughout the chapter he repeats the word \lquote wounded\rquote  as he's talking about Route 66. It's a place, he writes, where the cars become too hot, loose connections, loose bearings, rattling and here the cars also become a metaphor for the condition of the people, for the migrants who themselves often lose bearings as they're making that migratory journey from in the jobs case, Oklahoma to California. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So Route 66 here isn't marked with celebratory notions of American freedom, but with a sense of forced movement of people, which again contains echoes of the forced removal of Native Americans. The Joads get to California. They enter Route 66 Chapter 13, and many chapters later, after the deaths and many challenges along the way, they do arrive in California but of course, it's not the Promised Land that they had anticipated. The California that the Joads encounter is one shaped by labour conflicts and it's the experiences that Tom Joad and Ma Joad have as they negotiate their ways through the Hooverville camp and the Weed Patch camp, through the different jobs that they take up in California, the different migrants that they encounter and talk with, that they move towards an understanding of communalism and community and how that might be the way forward for humanity. Now it might be easy to assume that Steinbeck was a Marxist or socialist, and he certainly had socialist beliefs. But he was never a member of either organisation. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So what we're looking at are socialist values, not that Steinbeck is a socialist. Steinbeck's message in The Grapes of Wrath is helpfully understood by making a comparison to a novel that was published just one year before it. In 1938 Ayn Rand published her unsuccessful novel, Anthem and, in that text, Rand, who herself was a Russian immigrant to the United States champions Lassie Faire Capitalism and her short novel is a depiction of a journey from \ldblquote We\rdblquote  to \ldblquote I\rdblquote . This is a novel that celebrates individualism and the ego. Now Steinbeck is presenting the complete opposite to this in the novel. He's interested in the journey of \ldblquote I\rdblquote  to \ldblquote We\rdblquote , and it's that journey of \ldblquote I\rdblquote  two \ldblquote we\rdblquote  that I want to spend a bit more time thinking about now. To fully understand Steinbeck's notion of how we get from \ldblquote I\rdblquote  to \ldblquote we\rdblquote , we have to spend time thinking about Casey, who is an important character in this novel, in part because he has rejected formal religion and has taken up a more enlightened position as he questions humanity and how humanity can change going forward. \par
\par
 \par
\par
It's very early in the novel, when Tom meets Casey again in Chapter four, Casey begins to share some of his beliefs with Tom, and he tells Tom: \par
\par
 \par
\par
Maybe all men got one big soul ever\rquote body\rquote s a part of.\rquote  Now I sat there thinkin\rquote  it, an\rquote  all of a suddent\emdash I knew it. I knew it so deep down that it was true, and I still know it.\rdblquote  (26) \par
\par
 \par
\par
At the time,Tom is too absorbed in his own plight. He's desperate to get home to visit his family, so he isn't able to absorb Casey's message that all of humanity exists within one soul. It's only near the end of the novel and after Casey's death that Tom is able to understand Casey's true message. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So in his final conversation with Ma in Chapter 28 Tom says: \par
\par
 \par
\par
But now I been thinkin\rquote  what he said, an\rquote  I can remember\emdash all of it. Says one time he went out in the wilderness to find his own soul, an\rquote  he foun\rquote  he didn\rquote  have no soul that was his\rquote n. Says he foun\rquote  he jus\rquote  got a little piece of a great big soul. Says a wilderness ain\rquote t no good, \rquote cause his little piece of a soul wasn\rquote t no good \rquote less it was with the rest, an\rquote  was whole. Funny how I remember. Didn\rquote  think I was even listenin\rquote . But I know now a fella ain\rquote t no good alone.\rdblquote  (437) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Critics commonly read Casey's message within the American transcendentalist tradition of the 19th century, and particularly in relation to Ralph Waldo Emerson, who was a leading thinker of the transcendentalist movement in light of his idea of \ldblquote The Over-Soul\rdblquote . \par
\par
 \par
\par
So in 1841 Emerson wrote an essay called \ldblquote The Over-Soul\rdblquote .  \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote We live in succession, in division, in parts, in particles. Meantime within man is the soul of the whole; the wise silence; the universal beauty, to which every part and particle is equally related.\rdblquote  \par
\par
 \par
\par
And in that essay, Emerson wrote that \ldblquote within man is the soul of the whole\rdblquote , and we can see how that notion is embedded through The Grapes of Wrath, as both Casey and then Tom come to realise that mankind has the shared experience that brings everyone together under one soul or for Emerson, \ldblquote the soul of the whole\rdblquote . And inter-chapter 14 is one of the places in this novel where the narrator best explores the journey of \ldblquote I\rdblquote  to \ldblquote we\rdblquote  and this notion of a shared humanity. So, in inter-chapter 14, we learn: \par
\par
 \par
\par
The danger is here, for two men are not as lonely and perplexed as one. And from this first \ldblquote we\rdblquote  there grows a still more dangerous thing: \ldblquote I have a little food\rdblquote  plus \ldblquote I have none.\rdblquote  If from this problem the sum is \ldblquote We have a little food,\rdblquote  the thing is on its way, the movement has direction. Only a little multiplication now, and this land, this tractor are ours. The two men squatting in a ditch, the little fire, the sidemeat stewing in a single pot, the silent, stone-eyed women; behind, the children listening with their souls to words their minds do not understand. The night draws down. The baby has a cold. Here, take this blanket. It\rquote s wool. It was my mother\rquote s blanket\emdash take it for the baby. This is the thing to bomb. This is the beginning\emdash from \ldblquote I\rdblquote  to \ldblquote we.\rdblquote  (157 \endash  158) \par
\par
 \par
\par
As Steinbeck outlines this journey from \ldblquote I\rdblquote  to \ldblquote we\rdblquote , he repeats that word \ldblquote dangerous\rdblquote , that there's something dangerous for the status quo for the current economic situation. \par
\par
 \par
\par
If \ldblquote I\rdblquote  becomes \ldblquote we\rdblquote  it is something to bomb, something to destroy. So Steinbeck is underscoring how important it could be mankind to come together in this form of communalism. He captures this again in inter-chapter 14 with the line: \par
\par
 \par
\par
For the quality of owning freezes you forever into \ldblquote I,\rdblquote  and cuts you off forever from the \ldblquote we.\rdblquote  (158) \par
\par
 \par
\par
It's this line, perhaps more than any that underlines the message of Steinbeck's novel, that material possessions, financial success don't mean anything, that only coming together and working together in cooperation have any true communal sense of meaning. \par
\par
