 \par
\par
Probably the most obvious use of symbolism in Gatsby occurs in the opening paragraphs of chapter 2. There we are first introduced to The Valley of Ashes. One has to cross The Valley of Ashes to go from West Egg to Manhattan and it's a kind of Gothic terrain, the home of garage keeper George Wilson, who's wife Myrtle, when we first meet her: \par
\par
 \par
\par
walks past him as if he were a ghost. (28) \par
\par
 \par
\par
It's the inverse of a pastoral setting: \par
\par
 \par
\par
A fantastic farm where ashes grow like wheat into ridges and hills and grotesque gardens. (26) \par
\par
 \par
\par
It's a land of the living dead. Everything's made of ashes \endash  not least the working class: \ldblquote ash-grey men whose labor is obscured, by the \ldblquote impenetrable cloud\rdblquote  of ashes it raises. And then, of course, we have that billboard featuring the oculus Doctor. TJ Eckleburg. That billboard evokes both vision and blindness. It advertises someone who promises to help people see but also someone whom Nick suspects long ago shut down his business and \ldblquote sank down himself into eternal blindness.\rdblquote  It's tempting to read the symbol solemnly, and I'm sure many have, that is as the eyes of an indifferent or absent god or even an omnipresent god, but that would be to read the sign the way that George Wilson does after he's discovered his wife's infidelity, and he's quite distraught and he's saying to himself, \ldblquote You may fool me, but you can't fool God!\rdblquote  with his eyes on the sign. \ldblquote God sees everything.\rdblquote  But his comforter assures him that he's merely looking at \ldblquote an advertisement.\rdblquote  (152) \par
\par
 \par
\par
It would be hard, I think, to read Gatsby like TS Eliot\rquote s poetry, from which, arguably, the symbolism loosely derives, as earnestly informed by a sense of widespread, loss of religious belief but it does seem informed by widespread moral blindness and spiritual lethargy, which reduces people to ashes and dust. And yet what might redeem them in this novel? \par
\par
 \par
\par
The first description of Myrtle we should note singles her out by the fact that \ldblquote there was an immediate perceptible vitality.\rdblquote  (28) That's the key word, \ldblquote vitality\rdblquote  about her \ldblquote as if her the nerves of her body were continually smoldering.\rdblquote  (28) She's managed to stay alive while married to a ghost and surrounded by ashes. And we might recall in connection with this detail Nick's opening words in the novel in which he singles out Gatsby from the \ldblquote foul dust\rdblquote  around him. (8) Because Gatsby too has a certain vitality. He has, what Nick calls some \ldblquote heightened sensitivity to the promises of life\rdblquote  and \ldblquote an extraordinary gift for hope, and romantic readiness.\rdblquote  (8) Now one needn't draw a connection between those passages I've just done though, to see Myrtle as Gatsby's counterpart. She's in love with Tom, he's in love with Daisy. Both meet a violent death as a result \endash  she at the accidental hands of Daisy, though, you know, Gatsby will take the fall for that. He through the machinations of Tom, who will set Myrtle's vengeful husband on Gatsby\rquote s trail. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Both are finally preyed upon by the foul dust of the novel, the dust ultimately being the Buchanans, however they seem to float above it. However superficially they float about it, I think, Nick's ability to identify them with it is the hallmark of the correctness of vision he brings to writing the novel. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Myrtle is a very vibrant character. We get to know her before we meet Gatsby. She's in some ways kind of a comic version. We see her in a comic vein. She's in service to that same \ldblquote vast vulgar and meretricious beauty,\rdblquote  that Nick describes Gatsby as serving at the beginning of chapter 6 (95). Making a fortune and spending it lavishly, that's how Gatsby, a son of god, as Nick calls him, goes \ldblquote about his father's business.\rdblquote  (95) As Nick puts it in a very sharply ironic allusion to the gospel, according to Luke. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Myrtle, on the other hand, depends on Tom for her more modest spending sprees. En route to the apartment dhe buys the Town Tattle, a movie magazine, cold cream, perfume, she even buys a dog, after they've settled into this new one of the new taxis, the new lavender covered taxis, with gray upholstery. The apartment has oversized furniture with \ldblquote scenes of ladies swinging in the gardens of Versailles.\rdblquote  (31) Again, this kind of pretentious out of place decor aligns with some of the details of Gatsby's mansion. \par
\par
 \par
\par
I mean, another change of clothes changes my myrtle's personality, she suddenly is turned into a whole high born woman. So what we what we witness in chapter 2 is the transformation that money can bring with the help of a few commodities the vulgar Myrtle rereinvents herself into a high born lady. So, enacting on a smaller scale, and over the course of an afternoon, the essence of Gatsby's story. \par
\par
 \par
\par
We should be wary of reading the novel, though, as a moralistic expos\f0\'e9 of the shallowness of modern American consumerist materialism. Though the series of adjectives \ldblquote vast vulgar and meretricious\rdblquote  (95) grow increasingly negative, the noun they modify, \ldblquote beauty,\rdblquote  is unequivocally positive. And one aspect of Fitzgerald's brilliance as a writer, I think, was his attentiveness to the almost magical incandescent surface of modern American life. The alluring look of shiny new commodities and the pleasure-oriented places that showcase them. In that pre-Gatsby story, \lquote Absolution,\rquote  the crazy priest that Rudolph has to see urges young Rudolph to \ldblquote go see an amusement park at night.\rdblquote  (ch.5) And it's an apt symbol of American modernity, and its glittery Democratic appeal. We don't actually see one in the Gatsby, although at one point Gatsby does suggested Nick that they go to Coney Island but it's the central image, beneath those disembodied woman's eyes, on that Cugat cover, the original cover that Fitzgerald was very much drawn to and wanted for the book. And the image I think metaphorically captures the atmosphere at Gatsby's parties. There, as Nick describes a typical one in chapter three, \ldblquote The halls and salons and verandas are gaudy with primary colors, and hair shorn in strange new ways, and shawls beyond the dreams of Castile.\rdblquote  (42) \ldblquote The lights grow brighter as the earth lurches away from the sun. Cocktails sort of seemed to float in the air. The orchestra is playing yellow cocktail music.\rdblquote  42\rdblquote  But a party atmosphere and free flowing booze only intensifies what seems a permanent quality of much of the American scene, the contemporary American scene \f1\endash  again associated with New York and Long Island. Nick enjoys \ldblquote the racy adventurous feel of [New York] at night [\'85] the constant flicker of men and women and machines.\rdblquote  (57) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Driving across the Queensborough bridge shows, quote, \ldblquote city rising up across the river in white heaps and sugar lumps, all built with a wish out of nonolfactory money\rdblquote , \ldblquote a city promising all the mystery and the beauty in the world.\rdblquote  (67) And so Nick's seeing the city in the same way we learned Gatsby saw Daisy's house when he first entered it. This is again, a contrapuntal image that we see throughout the novel. This is Gatsby\rquote s impression of Daisy's house:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote There was a ripe mystery about it, a hint of bedrooms upstairs, more beautiful and cool than other bedrooms, of gay and radiant activities taking place through its corridors and of romances that were not musty and laid away already in lavender, but fresh and breathing and redolent of this year's shining motor cars and dances whose flowers were scarcely withered.\rdblquote  (141) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Daisy two gets affected by beautiful images. She's moved to tears by Gatsby stock of of beautiful shirts. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Shirts with stripes and scrolls and plaids and coral and apple green and lavender and faint orange with monograms of Indian blue. (89) \par
\par
 \par
\par
(Probably remember that Fitzgerald did some work in advertising before he became a successful writer.) All of this magic, I think, requires a certain vision, though, to sustain it. Otherwise, things do get reduced to ashes or become simply grotesque. When Gatsby's platonic conception of himself, and his incarnation of his dreams and all come crashing down, his once liberating faith in the unreality of reality takes an antithetical turn. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Nick imagines Gatsby's last moments waiting in his swimming pool for a phone call that never comes. And it becomes a horrifying revelation of a world depleted of any kind of human meaning and value: \par
\par
 \par
\par
He must have looked up at an unfamiliar sky through frightening leaves and shivered as he found what a grotesque thing a rose is and how raw the sunlight was upon the scarcely created grass. A new world, material without being real, where poor ghosts, breathing dreams like air, drifted fortuitously about\'85 like that ashen, fantastic figure gliding toward him through the amorphous trees. (153 \endash  154) \par
\par
 \par
\par
It's one of the most powerful descriptions in the novel, I think. I the world material with being without real. It's a world without any kind of human imagination invested in it. And after such a negative revelation, Gatsby is simply murdered by the character in the novel associated with the living dead. Fitzgerald does not finally hark back to the eyes of TJ Eckleburg to imply a judgment on godlessness. Rather he has Nick supply a secular mythical historical vision, that of the Dutch sailors coming westward, which I'll describe in a future lecture. That I think is used to kind of measure the meaning of Gatsby's failure to realize his platonic conception conceptions of himself in a contemporary America. But Nick is also troubled by more nightmarish visions in the aftermath of Gatsby's death. He's haunted not only by that sort of deanimated world of pure matter that Gatsby faces but a rather grotesque sordid social decadence that recalls: \ldblquote a night scene by El Greco\rdblquote  (167) and he's probably almost certainly thinking of El Greco's painting View of Toledo. Sarah Churchwell talks about in her essay on Gatsby in the new Cambridge companion. \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote After Gatsby's death, the East was haunted for me like that, distorted beyond my eyes\rquote  power of correction,\rdblquote  (167) Nick tells us. And so he returns back to the more traditional Midwest away from the racy adventure, the exciting flickering sites of New York City\rquote s \ldblquote vast vulgar and meretricious beauty\rdblquote  to find in words, in literary vision, whatever deeper redemptive meaning Gatsby's otherwise superficial and ultimately failed rags to riches story holds. \par
