 \par
\par
I think one of the most tenacious clich\f0\'e9s about Gatsby is that it's about The American dream.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
As Sarah Churchill's work reminds us in both her book Careless People and her recent essay in the Cambridge Companion, that term was not around when Fitzgerald wrote the novel. It does emerge soon after it, but it hardly connoted the unbridled individual pursuit of wealth.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
But the popular idea persists that The American Dream is about the pursuit of wealth and the enjoyment of a luxurious life. You know, such as the supposed happiness promised by the Declaration of Independence. The possibility of reinventing oneself usually factors into this but student responses to Gatsby almost always associate the American dream with wealth. Money, however, does not bring happiness to the characters in Gatsby. Gatsby seems to find happiness by winning Daisy again, but it's very short lived. That shouldn't leave us summing up the novel again in moralistic platitudes about the hollowness of The American Dream.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
I think a closer reading of the novel complicates our sense of what Fitzgerald's characters are seeking and missing, and why there seems to be no way out of their condition of fruitless pursuit. Any interpretation of Gatsby must take account of the closing paragraphs, Nick's quasi-mythic vision of the New World as first seen by those Dutch sailors, coming westward, the first Americans as it were. What this macro-historical vision of the Dutch sailors does is capture a kind of a paradigmatic experience, I would say, of desire and wonder, followed by possession and loss, that gets re-enacted on a kind of micro-historical scale by all subsequent Americans \f1\endash  Gatsby in the novel being the most representative American of his moment. The platonic conception of himself he seeks to realize is:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
just the sort of Jay Gatsby that a seventeen year old boy would be likely to invent.(95) \par
\par
 \par
\par
So again, informed by these circulating adolescent mythologies about what it would mean to be great. And we might remember that Gatsby's father brings that copy of Hopalong Cassidy and that you get the Ben Franklin esque schedule at the back. But the adult gatsby's most singular demand in life is that he can reverse time and return it to a perfect moment in his life when he suddenly had what he wanted. Only to lose it. So he wanted nothing less of Daisy were told, near the end of chapter six, then that she should go to Tom and I say, I never loved you. After she had obliterated four years with that sentence, they could decide upon the more practical measures, to be tagged, to be taken. One of them was that after she was free, they were to go back to Louisville and be married from her house. Just as if it were five years ago. But you can't repeat the past, says Nick. Can't repeat the past, Gatsby Christ. Well, of course, you can. He's incredulous, the idea that you can't repeat the past. What makes Gatsby stand out, I think, is mainly the determination he brings to doing the seemingly impossible. But Nick two seems haunted by an irrecoverable past. Hence, that wonderfully nostalgic recollection of coming back west from prep school and later from college at Christmas time. It's a very beautiful passage late in the novel. When he settles in West Steig at the beginning of the novel, though, he feels like a path finder, an original settler. Again, a kind of momentary identification with other first Americans. And he experiences quote that familiar conviction that life was beginning all over again with the summer. If there's an American dream in the novel, it seems to be precisely a dream of beginning over a game. Of returning to a perfect moment in time when one's idea of the good life lies before one. When paradise isn't already always already lost. Even Tom, even Tom Buchanan, is described as living in a condition of chronic anti climax. Nick imagines he would quote, drift for on forever seeking if a little wistfully for that dramatic turbulence of some irrecoverable football game. The climactic vision of the novel about the discovery, you know, the so called discovery of America, it offers an an origin myth of sorts. When Nick becomes again, this is beautiful passage I'll be quoting from. Aware of the old island here that once flowered for Dutch sailors' eyes, a fresh green breast of the new world. It's a pastoral feminine landscape, clearly. Seductive as well, as Nick imagines it kind of pandering and whispers to the last and greatest of all human dreams. For a transitory enchanted moment, Man must have held his breath in the presence of this continent compelled into an aesthetic contemplation. He neither understood nor desired, face to face for the last time in history, with something commensurate to man's capacity to wonder or to his capacity to wonder. Again, if there's an American dream in this novel, it's in this passage. The reference to the last and greatest of all human dreams. And what can this be but the deliverance from the corruption of human history? The chance to begin again. On a more egalitarian, more loving, and less instrumentalist footing. There's a potentially world transforming revelation here. But a transitory one. The Dutch sailors transcend all goals of possession and conquest and commerce. Being, again, keyword compelled into an aesthetic contemplation of the sublime magnificence of the new world. It's like a vision It's like a vision of god's promise. As that biblical phrase face to face might suggest. But from Nick's late American dissolution standpoint, it's the last time in history that a glimpse of paradise was granted. We know what followed. Landing, possession, conquest, commodification, the cutting down of trees to make way for houses like Gatsby's. No sooner is paradise found than it's lost. The American dream as envisioned here was doomed at its inception. History continues its corrupt course The dream of a new world simply succumbs to the practices of the old. The origin myth about the Dutch sailors implicit failure to act in accordance with their enchanted moment of breathless awe underlies the novel's ironic implication that Americans are in a deeper sense than Gatsby had in mind condemned to repeat the past. To trade in their wondrous dreams of Paradise and their platonic conceptions of themselves for things, for power for money, to lose them through the very means of of of seeking them. And on the micro historical level of the main story, Gadsby repeats the inevitable fall as it were of the Dutch sailors. So at the end of chapter six when Nick imagines Gatsby hovering in a state of enchanted suspension as the seductive Daisy offers up her mouth for a first kiss. He knew that when he kissed this girl and forever wed his unutterable visions to her perishable breath, his mind would never again romp like the mind of god. So he waited, listening for a moment longer to the tuning fork that had been struck upon a star. Then he kissed her. And at his lips, touch, she blossomed for him like a flower, and the incarnation was complete. Flowers, of course, wither after blossoming, and possession is prelude to loss. And of course, however wonderful a however wonderous a moment this passage captures. In the fuller context of the novel, we know precisely why Gatsby chose this particular woman to incarnate his visions, his his visions in. Her voice is full of money. Losing her, and especially this golden moment that he shares with her. He can only seek to recapture her. But even when he does, at the end of chapter five, Nick aptly surmises that there must have been moments even that afternoon when da daisy tumbled short of his dreams. Not through her own fault because of the colossal vitality of his illusion. Fitzgerald ends the note on a romantic ironic note. That is Americans seem destined. And, you know, I mean, I think he also imagines Americans representing humankind. That's fairly typical American literary tendency. Americans seem destined to pursue ideas of happiness that time, history, and death, the lie. Most of the characters in the Great Gasby are morally adrift in board, disillusioned like Nick or like George Wilson more dead than alive. Myrtle stands out for her vitality, though we'll end up dead on the side of a road. Her breast gruesomely ripped. And that was a detail fitzgerald wanted in there. Of course, and that rip breast, makes for an in ironic counterpart to the fresh green breast of the new world. Gatsby stands out for his vitality and more than that, his belief in the American promise metaphorically figured in the closing lines of the novel as the green light. The orgasmic future that year by year recedes before us. Scribner's wanted to correct Fitzgerald's word orgasmic to orgiastic, and Edmund Wilson actually did that in a nineteen forty reprint of the book. But fitzgerald, a letter from fitzgerald, indicates that it wasn't orgies he wanted to suggest Americans were chasing but something akin to orgasmic bliss. The stuff of a transitory enchanted moment, something that elusively promises to repair the damage of lost illusions. The loss of paradise can measure it with man's capacity to wonder. It eluded us then, but that's no matter. Tomorrow, we will run faster, stretch out our arms farther, and one fine morning, And Fitzgerald leaves this, leaves this penultimate sentence with this, long dash, a long dash, you know, a kind of an open line, and we might speculate from which those followers of the green light will eventually just fall off. But no one is any of the wiser. And thus, the famous final sentence of the novel So we beat on boats against the current, born back ceaselessly into the past. \par
