\par
 \par
\par
In his book, The Epic of America, James Truslow Adams defined The American Dream as I've been saying as something that offered \ldblquote a better, richer, happier life\rdblquote . Now, what a richer life might look like \endash  it's slightly complicated. One might assume that it's absolutely to do with money, wealth, but it's also about, in a broader sense, I think, of Rich Possibility in America. \par
\par
 \par
\par
I'm going to look here at F. Scott Fitzgerald's novel, The Great Gatsby, and then move on and think a bit about Tennessee Williams play, A Streetcar Named Desire. The Great Gatsby, published in 1926 dramatizes for many people, how the American dream was, at least to a certain extent, supposed to go. James Gatz of North Dakota, in his torn green jersey and pair of canvas pants, sees an opportunity to ingratiate himself with the multi-millionaire Dan Cody on his yacht. And he seizes it, instigating the reinvention of himself as Jay Gatsby, and setting himself on the eventual path to wealth and hosting all of those fabulous parties. But there are many tensions at the heart of the novel, not least between a man like Gatsby, who has apparently fulfilled the promise of the American dream, and embraced the idea of the new, and figures like Tom Buchanan and, to a certain extent, Nick Carraway the narrator. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Tom sees Gatsby as an outsider, and does everything he can to show that he cannot have access to the American dream because, as he often hints, he doesn't even consider Gatsby as American at all. In the tense, chapter seven said in the Plaza Hotel in New York City, Tom calls Gatsby, first of all, \ldblquote Mr Nobody from Nowhere\rdblquote , and then accuses him of trying to break up his marriage, arguing, apparently irrationally, that such an outrage is equivalent to intermarriage between black and white. It seems like an irrational comment, not connected at all to what they've just been talking about. But it's not the first time that Tom has suggested that somehow Gatsby is racially other. Tom\rquote s racist worldview cannot allow Gatsby access to wealth in America, and this echoes again that question we have to confront: \ldblquote who is allowed to access the ideals of The American Dream?\rdblquote  This leads in turn to a wider question: \ldblquote Who was allowed to be American in the 1920s?\rdblquote  I mentioned a little bit before the 1924 Immigration Act. The then president Calvin Coolidge, claimed that the act was necessary because, he said, \ldblquote America must be kept American\rdblquote . There was a real fear in the 1920s that foreigners or aliens were invading, not from other planets, but from different countries across the world and in 1924, the anti-black, anti-Semitic Ku Klux Klan or KKK homegrown terrorist group had more members than any other time in its history. They numbered approximately four million people. In contrast to Gatsby, people who do seem to fit into this universe, people like Nick, people like Tom, come from families of money and privilege. Tom, of course, is notoriously wealthy, and Nick's father supports him financially as he begins his career. If we go even further back in Nick\rquote s history, his great uncle is someone who's also described in terms of wealth, having sent a substitute to the American Civil War, something you could only do if you had enough money, since it involved paying someone else to fight and possibly die in your place. The American dream, an American nurse is often identified, then here and something only those with wealth and privilege can already access. It seems like a wrong way around rather than somebody coming from nothing to achieve something, here are people who already have money who are thinking about The American Dream might mean for them. Nick describes himself, in fact, early on in the novel, in terms of an American Dreamer: \ldblquote I was a guide, a Pathfinder, an original settler\rdblquote , as if he is one of those colonists or pioneers who sought to recreate themselves in the New World. The truth is, of course, that people like Nick, Daisy and Tom have had everything provided for them. However, the sense of a richer life through the Dream isn't just about money, it's about fulfilment. There's a great sense of possibility and optimism in the novel, from the symbolic imagery of the egg in the name of the place where Gatsby and Nick live to Nick\rquote s own name \ldblquote Carraway\rdblquote , plant, a seed with the potential to grow and of course he's not the only person in the novel who's named after something that grows: \ldblquote Daisy\rdblquote , \ldblquote Myrtle.\rdblquote  That sense of potential is never actually realised. Gatsby, Myrtle, Wilson all die. Nick's romance with Jordan and his romance with the East Coast of America falls apart and yet, even at the end of the book, that sense of possibility, so essential to our understanding of The American Dream is still there, though the dream is no longer just about Gatsby, it's about something much larger, as if Gatsby was just a kind of cipher through which other people might come to understand their own dreams. The ending of the novel is all about erasure and recalling, interestingly, a key aspiration of The American Dream: of being able to start from scratch to be able to start your life again from nothing. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Fitzgerald draws attention in the closing pages to Nick, erasing an obscene word on Gatsby's white steps, as if trying to return to an earlier time when nothing happened, and everything was pure white, innocent. And novel concludes like this: \par
\par
 \par
\par
And as the moon rose higher the inessential houses began to melt away until gradually I became aware of the old island here that flowered once for Dutch sailors\rquote  eyes \endash  a fresh green breast of the new world. Its vanished trees, the trees that had made way for Gatsby's house, had once pandered in whispers to the last and greatest of all human dreams. For a transitory enchanted moment man must have held his breath in the presence of this continent, compelled into an aesthetic contemplation he neither understood nor desired, face to face for the last time in history with something commensurate to his capacity for wonder. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The Great Gatsby, chapter 9 \par
\par
 \par
\par
Nick\rquote s vision of New World America is presented through the eyes of Old World Europeans as he attempts to capture again that sense of innocence. He does, in fact, what he told Gatsby wasn't possible because, at least in his mind's eye, he is repeating the past. Like earlier in the novel, we saw another hint of the potential of The American Dream. Perhaps it is possible to go back to an originary, intense, unsullied moment when sailors held their breath in wonder at what they saw. But, as I noted, in this section of the lecture about a better life, the past is inescapable. So much about The American Dream seems to be about looking forward. So much of it is also about looking backwards. Maybe Fitzgerald seems to be suggesting \endash  and this would be quite a radical thought \endash  that the dream is always retrospective and that America's best days are in the past, but no one wants to believe that. And in the story\rquote s final lines, Fitzgerald enacts that kind of compromise that, somehow, we can dream forward whilst being constantly pushed backwards.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
The American Dream always seems to occupy a space of tension between optimism and realism, a kind of nostalgic space where people long for a past that is gone but still believe the magic might happen again in the future. In a series of essays that he wrote called The Crack Up, which charted his own decline, published in 1936, Scott Fitzgerald wrote: \par
\par
 \par
\par
The test of a first-rate intelligence is the ability to hold two opposed ideas in the mind at the same time and still retain the ability to function. \par
\par
 \par
\par
F. Scott Fitzgerald, The Crack-Up (1926) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Perhaps the same can be said of The American dream. It pulls in two directions at once, but that doesn't necessarily make it any less attractive. Maybe The Great Gatsby does mark a moment in time when the party was really over and Jay Gatsby represented something in America that was disappearing and was finally extinguished by the Wall Street Crash in 1929 and The Great Depression that followed. Maybe it's all about a recognition that the kind of vast, vulgar and meretricious beauty Gatsby pursues is no longer sufficient. That word, meretricious, is a description by Nick that means something attractive but ultimately superficial, hollow. Given the disastrous years in American time, American history after Fitzgerald's novel was published, it's kind of understandable that Americans might want to replace that meretricious world with something that really had substance behind it. Something tangible and real as the world around them disintegrated during the Depression.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
In his play A Streetcar Named Desire, first performed in 1947, Tennessee Williams dramatizes another kind of struggle between two different worlds. The Old South, represented by Blanche Dubois and the New South \endash  even a new America, represented by Stanley Kowalski, who is Polish heritage but is not an immigrant, Blanche and her sister, Stella, who is married to Stanley. They are the last remaining members of the Dubois family, once sufficiently rich to own a plantation, \lquote Belle Reve,\rquote  literally \lquote beautiful dream\rquote  in French. But Stella left Belle Reve, married Stanley and now lives in New Orleans, very different kind of environment, and the plantation was lost when Blanche couldn't meet the mortgage payments. When she's challenged about it. Blanche hands the legal papers over to Stanley in scene two and, she says: \par
\par
 \par
\par
BLANCHE [picking up a large envelope containing more papers]:  \par
\par
There are thousands of papers, stretching back over hundreds of years, affecting Belle Reve as, piece by piece, our improvident grandfathers and fathers and uncles and brothers exchanged the land for their epic fornications \endash  to put it plainly!  \par
\par
[She removes her glasses with an exhausted laugh] Till finally, all that was left \endash  and Stella can verify that! \endash  was the house itself in about 20 acres of ground, including a graveyard to which now all but Stella and I have retreated. [She pours the contents of the envelope on the table] Here all of them are, all papers! I hereby endow you with them! Take them, peruse them, commit them to memory, even! I think it's wonderfully fitting that Belle Reve should finally be this bunch of old papers in your big, capable hands.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Tennessee Williams, A Streetcar Named Desire (1947), scene two \par
\par
 \par
\par
So if Belle Reve represents a certain version of The American Dream, it's one that's become an anachronism. It's out of place in the 20th century, morally bankrupt as well as financially so. The Dubois family was part of a patriarchal civilization that lived off slave labour until the Civil War and emancipation plunged that plantation living into crisis and the Old South, with its white column dimensions, fell into decline now, members of that civilisation and no more than ghosts haunting that graveyard. Handing over to the papers to Stanley might seem like a kind of gesture but even Blanche recognises that it is a symbolic one, going on to note that maybe Stanley is, she says,  \par
\par
 \par
\par
But maybe he\rquote s what we need to mix with our blood now we've lost Belle Reve and have to go on without Belle Reve to protect us.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Tennessee Williams, A Streetcar Named Desire (1947), scene two \par
\par
 \par
\par
It's an unconscious and uncomfortable foreshadowing of the sexual violence that Blanche should be subjected to by Stanley. Stanley is maybe a new kind of American. He tells Stella in scene eight that  \par
\par
 \par
\par
STANLEY \par
\par
When we first met, me and you, you thought I was common. How right you was, baby. I was as common as dirt. You showed me a snapshot of the place with the columns. I pulled you down off them columns and how you loved it, having them coloured lights going!  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Tennessee Williams, A Streetcar Named Desire (1947), scene two \par
\par
 \par
\par
 \par
\par
Stanley's narrative might seem to have the foundations of the American Dream \endash  is coming from nothing to establish himself in the world but unlike immigrants that I talked about in the first part of the lecture, Stanley is, as he says, is \ldblquote One hundred percent American, born and raised in the greatest country on earth and proud as hell of it.\rdblquote  This is a new kind of American Dream. Born to have a kind of entitlement. Stanley feels he's entitled to any wealth that might come from Stella's family through what he calls the \ldblquote Napoleonic Code.\rdblquote  He feels entitled to respect, perhaps especially respect, because he's a returning war veteran, after his service in World War Two, and he feels entitled to do whatever he likes as the King, as he puts it, of his home. But as in other texts, the idea of The American Dream also throws up questions about who is included and who is not and the epigraph to the play hints at the ways in which Blanche, in particular, struggles to fit in everywhere or anywhere. The epigraph is from Hart Crane's poem \lquote The Broken Tower\rquote ,  published in 1932 just after the author's death by suicide. The epigraph reads:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
And so it was I entered the broken world \par
\par
To trace the visionary company of love, its voice \par
\par
An instant in the wind (I know not whither hurled) \par
\par
But not for long to hold each desperate choice. \par
\par
 \par
\par
This part of the poem seems to set up the fantasies that we're going to see of Blanche. There's that famous claim she makes that she wants magic, not realism. But it also speaks to the sense that, in the play, we see Blanche\rquote s whole world being dismantled, broken in front of her, in front of us, with the audience. Crane\rquote s Poem explores a personal crisis actually, in his own life \endash  crisis of confidence that he could no longer write poetry with the kind of power he had successfully harnessed in the past, that his words would no longer obey him. And we can see this replicated and Blanche\rquote s situation. How much longer can she go on, moving from one desperate choice to another and sustain that dream like existence in a world that is seemingly left her behind? Crane was also a gay man at a time when he was often ignored, vilified or even violently attacked at times for being so. And \lquote The Broken Tower\rquote  can also be read as an attempt to consider homosexuality in light of his first ever sexual relationship with a woman. In this sense, he and his poem seemed undecided where she fit in. Can he fit in? What are the risks trying to do? Can Blanche and people like her live in the present? Will she / they always find themselves listening to the voices that haunt her? Those whispers in the wind that caused her to be borne back ceaselessly into the past. The play ultimately suggests there's no escape and there's no future for her. \par
\par
 \par
\par
