Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: The Inspiration for Huckleberry Finn \par
\par
Prof Mark Seybold \endash  Elmira College \par
\par
 \par
\par
Hi. My name is Matt Seybold. I am associate professor of American literature and Mark Twain studies at Elmira College, which is home of the Center for Mark Twain studies. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And today, we're going to be talking about Adventures of Huckleberry Finn by Mark Twain. \par
\par
 \par
\par
We are actually seated, or I am seated right now, in the study where Twain wrote Huck. Part of the role that the Center for Mark Twain Studies plays is the maintenance of this octagonal study in which Twain found his greatest literary productivity, producing several novels, travel books, stories, plays, essays, stand-up comedy routines, and much more over the course of a period of, more than twenty years, well more than twenty years. He spent his summers where we are, in Elmira, New York, working on whatever his project of the moment was, including during the late 1870s and early 1880s, the novel that we're talking about today, Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And I want to begin by locating the origins of that novel, the inspirations for that novel, including its relationship to an earlier period in American history and in Mark Twain's life, that is the American Civil War. After that, we will, discuss a little bit of the relationship between that novel and two of the dominant literary aesthetics of nineteenth century America. Those are realism and romanticism. And then we'll talk a little bit about the controversial ending of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, much debated especially in the contemporary period. And finally, we'll talk about the complicated afterlives of this novel and why it remains incredibly popular, and resonant, but also built upon, layers of kind of, political and social controversy. \par
\par
 \par
\par
As you may know, Adventures of Huckleberry Finn is, to some degree, a sequel to a previous novel, The Adventures of Tom Sawyer. And several characters and sites reappear, including Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry Finn themselves from that earlier work. And Twain certainly began writing Adventures of Huckleberry Finn with some of the intention to build upon the critical and commercial success of The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, but it very quickly became something altogether different and we're gonna discuss some of the potential reasons why or explanations for why that happened but I'm gonna focus first on an event, an event that happened here in Elmira, New York in August of 1880. \par
\par
 \par
\par
On August 3rd, 1880, the there was something called Emancipation Day, a celebration of the emancipation of enslaved peoples, not just in the United States, but also across the British Empire and Emancipation Day celebrations happened in various parts of the United States during the 1880s, but this was one of the first and one of the largest, and drew somewhere approximating ten thousand to fifteen thousand people from primarily the surrounding, area, northern Pennsylvania, western and central New York, the Finger Lakes region. \par
\par
 \par
\par
There were lots of events and, there was a lot going on during that day. There was a big parade. There were dances and banquets and music and all kinds of revelry, but it climaxed with a lengthy address, a kind of keynote speech given by Frederick Douglass. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Douglass was a formerly enslaved person who emancipated himself, in the 1840s and then became a very famous orator and abolitionist both before, during, and after the American Civil War, lived a relatively long life. He was invited to return to Elmira and, in fact, Elmira had been one of the places where he had hidden out during his initial escape and flight to what would become his kind of permanent home, which was Rochester, New York, which is about two hours north of here and, he was hosted, both during his return visits and also during his initial flight by what would become Mark Twain's in laws, his father-in-law and mother-in-law, Olivia Lewis Langdon and Jarvis Langdon. They, became, friends, associates, collaborators, some might say conspirators, in The Underground Railroad and Douglass devoted a great deal of gratitude, to them, in his later life, expressed an enormous amount of admiration for Jarvis Langdon, who by 1880 had died and Douglass, before he gave his speech, went to visit a kind of memorial to Langdon at the Park Church. Douglass's speech was about many things, but one of the things that it was about was the re-emergence of white supremacist power in the Southern states on in the aftermath of Reconstruction. \par
\par
 \par
\par
After the occupation of the Southern states ended and the reunion of North and South was allegedly accomplished, Douglass began to hear tell of and then to see for himself the emergence of what would come to be known as Jim Crow, but certainly as early as 1880 was recognizable as the sharecropping system and, one of the things he said in this long, it was almost a two hour speech, was: \par
\par
 \par
\par
Today, in most of the Southern States, the Fourteenth and Fifteenth amendments are virtually nullified. \par
\par
The rights which they were intended to guarantee are denied and held in contempt. The citizenship granted in the Fourteenth Amendment is practical practically a mockery, and the right to vote provided in the Fifteenth Amendment is literally stamped out in the face of Government. The old master class is totally triumphant, and the newly enfranchised class is in a condition but little above that in which they were found before the rebellion. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Frederick Douglass, 1 August 1880 \par
\par
 \par
\par
And he goes into a great deal more detail about what he was seeing and hearing about the emergence of Jim Crow and the sharecropping system in the Southern States. And this was a hard thing for many in the audience to hear. It was a predominantly black audience, filled with people who had fled, either before, during, or after The Civil War, some of whom undoubtedly still had relatives and friends and associates, who were living below the Mason Dixon line [regarded as the demarcation (together with the Ohio River) between the slave states south of it and the free-soil states north of it]. But it was a hard thing for the relatively smaller congregation of white auditors that were there, including Samuel Clemens, Mark Twain. And Twain considered himself a reconstructed Southerner, but he had not returned to the South since he left at the outset of the American Civil War. And it was very difficult, for reasons we'll discuss momentarily, it was very difficult for him to hear that the situation of formerly enslaved people, of emancipated people, was nearly as bad or as bad as it had been when he had himself been a Southerner and the son of a slaveholder. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And in the immediate aftermath of hearing Douglass's speech, he wrote something in his journal that I think is really important for us trying to grapple with Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. He said: \par
\par
 \par
\par
There is not a single celebrated Southern name in any of the departments of human industry except those of war, murder, assassination, lynching, the duel, repudiation, and massacre. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, Notebook 19 (1881) \par
\par
 \par
\par
For Twain, this was not just a moment of anger towards his Southern heritage and towards towards the Southern communities with which he still, to some degree, identified. It was also a just certain amount of anger towards himself and he went on to write that there is not a single celebrated Southern name in the arts or sciences, suggesting that he did not consider himself, despite being an incredibly successful and famous writer by this point, to have been a successful Southerner in however he wanted to define that. And then, just a couple days later, he was clearly grappling with Douglass's address. Just a couple days later, he finally booked passage to return to the South.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
And in 1881, he went back to the Mississippi River. He toured up and down, and up and down again. He stayed several months in, in several of the states, Mississippi, Louisiana, certainly Missouri, Arkansas, witnessing the situation of the border South and the Deep South now a decade and a half removed from emancipation. He was ostensibly there to, prepare his memoir of his time as a Mississippi River boat pilot, a book that would come out, a year later called, Life on the Mississippi. But immediately upon returning to Quarry Farm and to this study, he would pick up Adventures of Huckleberry Finn again and, over the next two summers, would write furiously the back half or two thirds of the novel, Clearly deeply informed by his own return to the South. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The actor and Twain impersonator, Hal Holbrook, would argue that that trip and, in particular, seeing the conditions of formerly enslaved and allegedly emancipated people is what makes Adventures of Huckleberry Finn into a candidate for The Great American Novel. \par
\par
 \par
\par
{{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://massolit.io/courses/twain-huckleberry-finn-seybold/the-inspiration-for-huckleberry-finn }}{\fldrslt{https://massolit.io/courses/twain-huckleberry-finn-seybold/the-inspiration-for-huckleberry-finn\ul0\cf0}}}}\f1\fs22   \par
