\par
Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: Twain and The American Civil War \par
\par
Prof Mark Seybold \endash  Elmira College \par
\par
 \par
\par
At the end of the last section, I mentioned how Twain returned to the American South in 1881 for the first time since he had fled at the outset of the Civil War in 1861. And so he had spent a full twenty years away from both what had been his childhood home and also would be the setting of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, Puddnhead Wilson, Life on the Mississippi, many of his most popular narratives and novels. \par
\par
 \par
\par
It's very hard to overstate how important the Civil War is for understanding both Mark Twain's overall literary career and the specific novel Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. On the one hand, it's a novel set twenty years before the American Civil War, and no part of that conflict is actually represented literally within the novel. However, it's a novel that is written, published twenty years after the American Civil War and so that conflict sits as a kind of ghost, right, between the story being told and the book being published. And there's a variety of things that we might like to know about Twain and his circumstances and his understanding and interpretation of the war that can help us to understand the novel. \par
\par
 \par
\par
I'll start by acknowledging that although Twain characterized himself as a short term combatant who joined with an upstart militia in the early months following the outbreak of the Civil War and following The Declaration of Secession by the southern states, he did not participate in any combat and was likely or at least as part of that militia, he did not participate in any combat and was likely only with them to bide his time until he could escape and flee westward and he spent the remainder of the actual combat period of the American Civil War out west in Nevada and California. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The reason why he was looking to evade, was that when the war broke out, when the war was declared, he was a steamboat pilot on the Mississippi River. And the Mississippi River was immediately an artery of extreme tactical importance. One of the first things the Union Army did was secure the Mississippi River. And Twain was working as a steamboat pilot during that campaign and was commandeered. Right? He was forced into labor for the Union Army. He was conscripted and made to work at least two passages up and down the river for a steamboat that had been requisitioned by the Union Army. And during that time, not only was his boat fired upon and he felt endangered by the potential for some skirmish. But he also, as he described it, spent much of the time with somebody with a gun sort of lording over him, telling him what to do, watching him, obviously afraid of who his loyalties were to. And he returns. He actually flees under cover of darkness and returns to Saint Louis and hides out in a state of extreme anxiety and panic as his family members would describe it. And it is during this time that he concocts the plan to join with his brother, who has a kind of minor, government post out west and to flee the active combat of the Civil War. And so Twain has this sort of very early recognition as very few other Americans of his generation of that time had, of just how dangerous this war might prove to be. And, of course, that, you know, that proves to be the case. And it becomes the most deadly conflict in American history, somewhere in the vicinity of 750,000 casualties. And, and Twain feels that very directly. He loses, friends and extended family members. His, family has to relocate somewhere else and, maybe most directly, he loses what he thought was going to be his lifelong career. He loved being a steamboat pilot. He liked it a lot better than being a printer or a journalist, two trades which he had tried before he became a pilot and which he would return to when he fled to Nevada and California. So he has direct familiarity and consequence from this war. And, of course, the country, he comes to understand, although it takes him, I would say, at least a decade or so, he comes to understand just how impactful the conflict is for both his family and his communities, but also for the country at large. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And the long term psychological and social ramifications of even setting aside enslavement, which we will talk about in a moment, of just the loss of that many young men of a certain generation. I tell my students that if you are of their age, in their late teens and early twenties, roughly 1 out of every 3 of them would have died during the American Civil War. It's hard for us to imagine a conflict of that consequence and the kind of psychological ramifications, social ramifications, structural ramifications of the American Civil War. And it takes Twain some time to wrap his head around it as well. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And so I say all this to come back to that trip where he goes back to the South, where he hears Frederick Douglass speak about the re-emergence of a system that is only a slight step above enslavement. And Twain returns to see it for himself, and has to grapple with the idea that all the stuff that he went through, that his family and friends went through, that the country went through, that enslaved and emancipated people went through, all of these things which he had been sort of wrapping his mind around over the course of two decades, that they could only really be justified and rationalized by the claim that many abolitionists had been making and had made, that it was all worth it, right, to end the angry scar on American identity, which was ongoing black enslavement. \par
\par
 \par
\par
If that wasn't ended, then was any of it worth it? \par
\par
 \par
\par
And I think in some ways, the darkness of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, a novel which is quite violent, which is filled with corpses, which is filled with cruelty, which is filled with horror. That darkness is very much a product of Twain's recognition that for all of the death and destruction of lives that had taken place both during enslavement and then during the conflict itself of the Civil War: nothing had changed, or at least had not adequately changed. And I think this is a really crucial element for our understanding of what he's trying to accomplish in Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. \par
\par
 \par
\par
