Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: The Epitome of Social Realism \par
\par
Prof Mark Seybold \endash  Elmira College \par
\par
 \par
\par
We have moved now to the Mark Twain reading room at Elmira College, a room that is comprised of a lot of the materials that we found in the tavern that Mark Twain frequented, Claproth Saloon, when he was living in Elmira, but it which also includes the books that he read and annotated and left at Quarry Farm. And it seems a fitting place to talk about Twain's relationship to literary aesthetics in the late nineteenth century. In particular, I want to talk now about American Realism, and the phenomena of literary realism with which Twain and in particular Adventures of Huckleberry Finn is often very closely associated. \par
\par
 \par
\par
One of the primary reasons for that association has to do with the narrator, with the first person dialect storytelling of Huck Finn and that one way of thinking about realism is that what makes it real is that we are actually going inside the mind and perspective of a character and trying to understand the world through the peculiar lens of that character's psychology. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And realism is often associated, at least in America with writers like William Dean Howells and Henry James but also, despite the fact that his subject matter and his characters are very different from James', also with Mark Twain and particularly with Adventures of Huckleberry Finn because Huck turns out to be a kind of epitome of the realistic narrator. Somebody who we get to know really well. The way that his mind works, and we get to experience the world through him. And yet, he's not particularly reliable. We know for a fact that he has a capacity for imitation, for impersonation, that he has very little trouble lying spontaneously, That he also is very young and sometimes somewhat na\f0\'efve and doesn't necessarily trust his own instincts even when he should. And therefore, as readers, we are placed in this position where we are both utterly and fully dependent upon his worldview, his, his account of events, his perspective, and yet we are constantly also doubting to some extent his capacity to yield a kind of verisimilitudinous account of what's happening around him. To see the ways in which he might be manipulated, right, might be conned, right? And then also to maybe withhold from us at times things that he feels ashamed about, or that he is dissembling about in some way, shape, or form. So one reason why Adventures of Huckleberry Finn is often characterized as a kind of epitome of the American realist novel has to do with the peculiar character and narrative perspective of Huck. \par
\par
 \par
\par
But I want to talk also about thinking about the way the structure of the novel contributes to another kind of definition of realism. One that's definitely related to the narrative perspective, that's definitely related to Huck's psychology but also has some distinctly American elements to it. And this definition of realism, I would say, is that the primary objective is to capture the combustible configuration of contradictions and competing claims, which is the inevitable result of Americans attempting to live according to their own cultural and constitutional principles. And in many ways, I think this is at the centre of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, that the novel begins subtly as a kind of allegory. We spend the first ten chapters in St Petersburg, in small-town Missouri, that becomes a kind of microcosm of antebellum America. That we have a set of characters who are exacting their influence and impressions upon Huck that are stand-ins for cultural institutions and ideologies. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Some of the obvious ones here, we could think about Widow Douglas, who adopts Huck, and, who he's very fond of, very indebted to, but he also feels, to some extent, entrapped by a lifestyle that is utterly unfamiliar to him. And the widow Douglass stands in for Protestant Christianity, stands in for the church. She's very devout, she's sometimes a little bit sanctimonious, and she is trying to impress upon Huck the importance of going to church, of participating in organized religion, of faith and good works, and the various, ideas associated with Protestant Christianity, all of which are both unfamiliar to Huck and which he has a kind of knee jerk scepticism about, although he recognizes that everybody around him, at least ostensibly, shares these beliefs to some degree or another. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Another figure we meet is Miss Watson, a school teacher by trade, also a very generous and loving person, although a little bit stricter than the Widow Douglas, who sort of stands in for the institution of the school and this broader ideology of liberalism or liberal education, that she believes that the training of the mind in things like literacy, in particular, mathematics, in a certain version of Western history, that this will create capacities for Huck that will make it easier for him to move through the world, despite the fact that he has very limited resources, that he is, at least for a period, an orphan and becomes an orphan again, that, the widow and Miss Watson stand for the church and the school, and sometimes those things being at odds with one another. \par
\par
 \par
\par
We also have Judge Thatcher who is the most powerful man in Saint Petersburg, and who stands in not just for the courts, as his name suggests, but is the person who the boys turn to for advice about both politics and economics. He is a figure of democratic capitalism. Of the structure of American governance that is at least somewhat unique to this nation in those antebellum days. \par
\par
 \par
\par
But it's also deeply flawed, okay, in ways that we will discover or that Huck will discover along the way. And then let us not forget Pap Finn. Although he is the villain of the early stages of this novel, certainly, and the figure who most overtly expresses the ideology of white supremacy, although I think we come to see probably all of the people of St. Petersburg share that to some degree. Pap also has some things to teach Huck. In particular, self reliance and rugged individualism. That one of the values of early America is this kind of frontier ethos, this stampede into the wilderness. And Pap Finn, amongst the characters we meet in the early sections of this novel, other than Jim and then Huck, Pap is the only one who can really make it on his own out in the wilderness. The kind of things that Huck needs to be able to do once he and Jim are out on the raft. Things like building a fire and pitching a tent and fishing. And even hiding out. These are things that he learns to do, at least in part, through his association with this ne'er do well father. And these are also definitely values that are deeply and intrinsically associated with the American character in the early nineteenth century. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So, we have this sort of set of allegorical relationships that are imposing themselves upon Huck during the first ten chapters of the novel very directly, and then, and this is the real value of a first person narrator, we get to experience him rethinking and also regurgitating the ideas that are impressed upon him by the people of Saint Petersburg, trying to reconcile them to one another and also to the experiences he's having once he's out on the river, once he's viewing the world apart from the microcosm of Saint Petersburg. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And there's one last character that I want to make sure we mention and that's Tom Sawyer, Huck's best friend, who is the only of these characters who we are going to see again later in the novel other than Jim who remains with him throughout. He's the only person from Saint Petersburg who's going come back, and come back in in interesting ways. And Tom is the figure of romance, the figure of chivalry, the figure of literature. His institution is the library. Tom is formed far less by the schoolhouse and the church than he is by his own intrepid and extensive reading of what we might characterize as pulp fiction. Adventure novels about pirates and knights and a whole range of characters and types, all of which are very clearly situated in the romantic tradition. A kind of instructive literature that creates worlds that are coherent. And Tom takes those worlds and tries to project them into Saint Petersburg and into Huck's life. And Huck is very moved and influenced and oftentimes, treats Tom as his closest friend and somebody to whom he is willing to become a kind of side kick. And I think Tom\rquote s allegiance to the romantic tradition is very important for thinking about how Huck is going to transform and resist the ideologies that are impressed upon him in St. Petersburg, and also how realism is going to merge over the course of this novel. \par
\par
 \par
\par
 \par
\par
