Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: The End of American Romanticism \par
\par
Prof Mark Seybold \f1\endash  Elmira College \par
\par
 \par
\par
While many critics have described Adventures of Huckleberry Finn as either the origin or the epitome of American Realism, I'm more inclined to think about it as an attack on, perhaps even an attempt to end American Romanticism. \par
\par
 \par
\par
I closed the last section talking a little bit about Tom Sawyer, who I think of as the sort of personification of Southern Romanticism. Somebody who is absolutely obsessed with reading and then trying to recreate novels about highwaymen and pirates and, cowboys, and frontiersmen, and also knights and aristocrats. That he is embracing a novelistic romantic tradition that, as far as Twain was concerned, was most closely associated with Walter Scott.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
You may remember from an earlier section how I described the importance of the American Civil War and its legacy for Twain's composition of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. During that same trip to that return trip to the South, Twain came up with this thesis, which he describes most extensively in Life on the Mississippi, that Walter Scott was, perhaps, the person most responsible for the American Civil War. He calls it the Walter Scott disease. And the way he rationalizes this is that Southern slaveholders, particularly those who were actively and directly involved in the plantation system, used Walter Scott's novels and a variety of both English and American novelists who were imitating Scott as a way of constructing a kind of mock feudalism, a mock gentry or aristocracy around the plantation system. And that they created a kind of aesthetic, a set of rituals, forms of dress, false military titles, that allowed them to kind of play act aristocracy. And by so doing, imagine themselves as, as kind of lords and ladies and superiors to not only the enslaved people on whom their wealth was based, but also to the plebs and serfs, right, who were not part of that plantation system. And as far as Twain is concerned, this aesthetic tradition of American romanticism is absolutely essential to maintaining a set of political delusions that allow enslavement to persist and then also lead to Southerners believing that secession is a good idea and that they can win the Civil War on the basis of a kind of heroism and chivalry that is part of the kind of landscape of adventure novels. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And while Twain gives us this thesis in great detail in Life on the Mississippi, it is also clearly very influential on Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. And indeed, we have some sort of symbols like the sunken steamboat that is named The Walter Scott, kind of indicate some of the ways in which Twain is going to attack this tradition of American Romanticism. \par
\par
 \par
\par
I believe that comes to a climax in chapter eighteen, in the Grangerford and Shepherdsons episode, where we actually find ourselves embedded in directly in the plantation system to a degree, that's not the case in a border South town like Saint Petersburg. Huck spends a kind of indeterminate period of time, probably weeks and possibly even months, separated from Jim and embedded in the Grangerford home, where he gets accustomed to the affectations and the rituals and the ideologies that are associated with this plantation system, where he sees the kind of feudal pretences that the Grangerfords have put on, but he also sees the ludicrous amount of violence that is going to be justified by those pretences. And in particular, the way that Twain tells this story, through the the feud between the Grangerfords and the Shepherdsons where these all very wealthy, very entitled, otherwise seemingly very civilized people have no qualms about killing each other, including young men, killing each other, killing, children, In the name of a long feud, the origins of which nobody in the family can really articulate or remember. And, of course, this chapter climaxes with Huck being stuck in a tree and watching the sort of final confrontation between the Grangerfords and Shepherdsons, where his avatar, a young man named Buck, is, brutally murdered in front of his eyes. And thus, we have Huck coming face-to-face with the reality of the kinds of illusions of romanticism and chivalry and heroism, and aristocracy, which Tom Sawyer had taught him to idealize. Huck gets to see the kind of worst case scenario of those ideas playing out in the real world, and the sort of senseless violence and death which is the result of them. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And for me, the argument I will make is that Twain is trying to kill Romanticism in that tree in Orion County in the middle part in the eighteenth chapter of Huckleberry Finn. He's trying to draw attention to the damage that a certain type of literary aesthetic can do. The illusions and delusions that it creates and the real violence that is the result of those illusions. And that's going be very important, not just for Huck coming to terms with the possibility that many of the things that he thought were good and right and true, or at least that people from Saint Petersburg had been telling him were good and right and true, are, in fact, suspect, are perhaps even damaging, including, of course, potentially, the institution of enslavement and the ideology of white supremacy upon which it depends. That we have immediately after the Grangerford and Shepherson's feud, Twain brings Huck and Jim back together. And for the first time, Huck treats Jim as a family member. As somebody with whom he feels a great degree of intimacy and familiarity, and upon whom he's willing to depend and trust, and who he sees is, despite his race, a far more wise and genuine and trustworthy, and rational person than any of the very wealthy and well-established and reputable people who he has been spending time with in the Grangerford home. \par
\par
 \par
\par
