Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: The Ending of the Novel \par
\par
Prof Mark Seybold \endash  Elmira College \par
\par
 \par
\par
We're gonna wrap up these lectures talking about the end of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, and then the afterlives of the novel. And we're doing so from the porch at Quarry Farm, the property on which Mark Twain's study was originally located and, thus, where Adventures of Huckleberry Finn was predominantly composed. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And the thing I wanna say about this location before we get back to Huckleberry Finn is that in eighteen seventy four, roughly ten years before Twain completed Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, he it was here on this exact porch that he listened to a formerly enslaved woman by the name of Mary Ann Chord, who became the live in cook here at Quarry Farm, tell the story of being separated from her children during a human auction in North Carolina and then reunited with her youngest son during Sherman's march through the Carolinas in at the at the end of the civil war. \par
\par
 \par
\par
A story that deeply moved Samuel Clemens and his wife, Olivia, and which he immediately set about trying to recreate in prose What would become, published in the Atlantic Monthly about eight months later, the true story repeated word for word as I heard it. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And this story, I think, is really important both for Twain's career and specifically for Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. For one thing, it's the first story that Twain ever published in the Atlantic Monthly, which was the most prestigious literary magazine in America at that time. It was also as he said, it was the first story he had ever written that had no humor in it, and therefore presented him with the possibility of thinking of himself as a writer, though, who could be very different from the kind of burlesque humorist and satirist that he had been up to that point. \par
\par
 \par
\par
It was also Twain's first experiment with black dialect, right, his first attempt to render the voice of a black character, something that he would continue to work on very fastidiously over the next decade, in which, of course, he would make reference to in the preface to Adventures of Huckleberry Finn that his one of the goals of the novel was to render, accurately the range of dialects that one would encounter in the border south amongst both white and black peoples. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And so it's here that he he really starts thinking about a kind of literary creativity and particularly a kind of literary representation that is dramatically different from what he had been doing up to that point, and is also clearly, dramatically influential upon what kinds of techniques and experience and talent he could foster and then bring to an ambitious novel like Adventures of Huckleberry Finn. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The perhaps most consistently controversial aspect of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn is its ending. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The final ten chapters after what is the kind of moral climax of the book when Huck writes, a letter to miss Watson, in which he confesses to being complicit in Jim's self emancipation. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And then he tears up that letter and says, alright then, I'll go to hell. Essentially, acknowledging that combustible confer, configuration of contradictions and competing claims that have been working upon him for so many chapters that he is going to deny, right, the what he believes to be not just the law of the land, but also God's law, in order to sustain some kind of allegiance or loyalty or friendship to Jim. Mhmm. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And it's a it's a beautiful, epiphonic moment in the novel. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And some critics, including the twentieth century novelist Ernest Hemingway, have said it really should be the end of the book. And Hemingway says that you should stop reading at that point. The rest is just cheating. But that's not where Twain stops. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And it's really important for us to think about why he tacks on this ten chapter ending in which we have the return of Tom Sawyer, we have the at least temporary re enslavement of Jim, We have Huck falling into the role of a side kind of sidekick, and, more beholden to Tom and the Phelpses than he is to Jim. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And I think there's a few ways to read this ending. \par
\par
 \par
\par
One of those ways is as a failure and a betrayal. Right? \par
\par
 \par
\par
And Twain essentially giving up on the potential antiracist elements in the book and returning to a kind of burlesque humor that places Jim in a position of submission and, race comic racism, and and many critics have read it that way. \par
\par
 \par
\par
But I also wanna present some some alternatives. \par
\par
 \par
\par
One certainly has to do with that death of romanticism that I talked about earlier. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The attempt to reveal the violence and debasement implicit in the romantic tradition through the murder of Buck Grangerford while Huck is watching from the tree in Orion County. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And I think one way to understand what happens at the end is that we have Tom Sawyer, the personification of romanticism, returning to the narrative, and then playing out, reenacting the plots of some of Walter Scott's most famous novels with Jim as the the imprisoned person who is trying to escape and is having to go through all kinds of torturous escapades in order to escape. And so we have the return of the personification of romanticism, But whereas in the Adventures of Tom Sawyer and even in the first ten ten ten chapters of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, Tom's play acting of romantic plots seems relatively innocent and childish and idyllic. When he starts to do it at the end of Adventures of Tom Sawyer, we can't but see it as cruel and dehumanizing. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And Tom becomes a kind of villain, right, whose delusions, and his sense of entitlement, and his lack of ability to see the harm and pain that he is causing Jim is very recognizable to the readers who have come to develop an affection as is the kind of betrayal that Huck undergoes. Right? He has this this moment where he says he is going to participate in, in securing Jim's freedom by any means necessary, even at the cost of his own immortal skull. But then he falls into the role as Tom's sidekick. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And I believe, right, all of this is very intentional, that Twain needs us to recognize that Huck is not a hero. Mhmm. That Huck is somebody who's only a few stratums above enslavement himself, who has no real systemic power over the society in which he lives, and is almost immediately a, subject of his social relationships, his associations, his material conditions, right, that he hasn't either the courage nor the competency as much as we might come to admire him to overcome the circumstances that he finds himself in at the end of the novel. Right? \par
\par
 \par
\par
Twain also needs to bring back some of the forms and tropes of Walter Scott's romanticism in order to show how ludicrous they are. Right? That we have him taking a kind of corpse of romanticism that is now recognizable to readers as cruel and violent and debased, and parading it across the stage in the final eight chapters or the final ten chapters in a way that makes it impossible, arguably, for us to return to the delusions that one might have associated with the antebellum plantation romance. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And so on the one hand, what he's doing is furthering his implicit critique of American romanticism. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Also, this is the result of what he saw when he returned to the south in eighteen eighty one after, listening to Frederick Douglass tell him about the re, the return to power of the old master class and the emergence of the sharecropping system is that Reconstruction had failed. \par
\par
 \par
\par
That, the kind of emancipatory abolitionist narrative of the end of the Civil War was a myth. And the end of the novel has to be tragic because the failure of reconstruction is itself tragic. Right? That this is Twain's way of allegorizing, what was happening in the country around him. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Right? That there is if if emancipation and abolition and the path towards a post racial, you know, non white supremacist America had been accomplished, then there would be no point in writing a novel about enslavement twenty years after the civil war. But what he saw when he returned to the South was that many of the conditions persisted, and certainly, the prejudice and discrimination and the violence built upon that prejudice and and discrimination continued. Alright? \par
\par
 \par
\par
And that's what we have happen in at the end of the novel as well. Right? Is that we have Jim reenslaved in in practice, right, if not in legal terms, right, which was exactly what was happening to black people in the aftermath of Reconstruction. And and so from my perspective, the novel's ending is an intentional failure. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Alright? That it's designed to make us uncomfortable. Right? To make us experience as as Toni Morrison calls it, it's a simulation. \par
\par
 \par
\par
It's a simulation of the failure of Reconstruction and of the endurance of white supremacy. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And Huck can't win. Okay? He can't go to hell. \par
\par
 \par
\par
At least in the way that he, conceives it during that, that moral climax ten chapters earlier. \par
\par
