Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: The Afterlife of Huckleberry Finn \par
\par
Prof Mark Seybold \endash  Elmira College \par
\par
 \par
\par
I'm going to conclude just briefly talking about the afterlife of this novel in American culture. Huckleberry Finn was a success in Twain's lifetime. It was a commercial success. It was, to a slightly lesser degree, a critical success. \par
\par
 \par
\par
It certainly had controversies associated with it from the very start. It was banned from a library in Massachusetts in particular for its depiction of children and childhood. That Huck was not an admirable young man. He was not a well-educated young man. He was not somebody who the board of the Concord Library thought their young readers should be emulating and thus, he was, as Twain put it, thrown out of the venerable institution of the Concord Library. So it was a controversial book from the very start, but not really in the ways that it became in the latter half of the twentieth century. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And I think in order to fully understand the kind of hold over the American literary canon, which Huckleberry Finn came to have, we have to talk about the embrace of a kind of mythological Twain that happens during The Cold War, during the post World War Two period in the United States. That Twain is kind of held up as an iconic American cultural figure, and because of the propagandistic media environment of the Cold War period, his life and works and reputation have to be moulded to fit into the myths of America that are both being proffered domestically and exported abroad. And this is happening in a wide variety of ways through cultural exchanges, through Disneyfication of Tom Sawyer, through board games and Western film and television and so on and so forth. What I have sometimes referred to as \lquote The Twain Doctrine,\rquote  where both government agencies and educational institutions and private corporations were all involved in creating a kind of iconography of Mark Twain and his works, which would serve to promote a particular version of nationalism that was suitable to the geopolitical interest of the United States during the Cold War period. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Part of that, one part of that, is what Jonathan Arac refers to as hypercanonization. That is that Huckleberry Finn, for whatever reason, was chosen as the Twain novel that would be canonized to a degree that almost no other American novel had ever been, that it would become required reading in almost every high school across the country, even for a time in many junior highs. And so it was very difficult for students throughout the latter half of the twentieth century to exit high school, exit secondary school without having read the novel at least once, or been assigned the novel at least once and probably two or three times. Right? That it was going to be by far, at least for a period of decades, it was going to be by far the most assigned text in American public schools, and that would continue in higher education as well. And so, for, generations of American students, Huckleberry Finn was held up as a kind of the great American novel. \par
\par
 \par
\par
And a particular interpretation, what I would argue is a very flawed and limited and certainly nationalistic interpretation, which totally denied the arguments about the ending, which I, presented in the last section, which essentially presented Adventures of Huckleberry Finn as a parable of America overcoming the sins of its inception. That Huck stands in for America, recognizing the humanity of black people and then giving them their freedom, and thus becoming the exceptional ideal, national, almost Christ figure that America was always destined to be. That's the kind of version of Huckleberry Finn that was most often taught in American schools during the second half of the twentieth century. It's not a version that's necessarily very reconcilable to careful reading of the novel. It requires a very selective choosing of chapters, excerpts, scenes and passages, and it required a lot of work on behalf of instructors and professors to hold up this particular version of Huckleberry Finn that starts to fall apart in the 1990s. And I think in large part because of this kind of habitual, propagandistic, flawed reading of Huckleberry Finn, it gained a sort of renewed controversy in the late very late 20th century and 21st century as no longer under the spectre of that particular Cold War ideology, people came to recognize that the novel was not doing what many critics had been claiming and teachers had been claiming it was doing, and also that it had a much more tortured, complex and pessimistic or cynical view of both America and white supremacy than maybe they had been told it had. And so I think we can't talk about Adventures of Huckleberry Finn without acknowledging this, the role that hyper-canonization played in presenting it as the kind of emblematic novel of American exceptionalism during the second half of the twentieth century. \par
\par
 \par
\par
It doesn't need to be read that way. In fact, it shouldn't be read that way. But for a long time, it was. And it bears the weight of that critical corpus just as much as it does the actual reception of it during its own time. \par
\par
 \par
\par
