Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: The Language of America \par
\par
Prof Larry Howe \par
\par
Roosevelt University \par
\par
 \par
\par
Before Adventures of Huckleberry Finn gets underway, the readers encounter two prefaces. First, a notice, which Professor Ruys Smith mentioned in his lecture series. And second, a preface that Twain calls an \ldblquote Explanatory.\rdblquote  It reads as follows: \par
\par
 \par
\par
In this book, a number of dialects are used. To wit, the Missouri negro dialect, the extremest form of the backwoods South-Western dialect; the ordinary \ldblquote Pike-County: dialect; and four modified versions of this last. The shadings have been have not been done in a hap-hazard fashion, or by guess-work; but pains-takingly, and with the trustworthy guidance and support of personal familiarity with these several forms of speech. \par
\par
I make this explanation for the reason that without it many readers would suppose that all these characters were trying to talk alike and not succeeding.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Signed the author. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (4) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now while the last sentence is no doubt tongue in cheek, the main idea of the Explanatory points to Mark Twain's interest in the diverse forms of American speech and especially the speech of people who live at a distance from the major Eastern cities of the United States, which dominated American culture. \par
\par
 \par
\par
For many readers, Twain's greatest achievement was his representation of authentic American dialects. To understand the significance of American speech, we need to go back to the founding of the United States as an independent nation. Shortly after the revolution and continuing into the nineteenth century, there were repeated calls for an American literature and culture that distinguished itself from that of Great Britain. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Noah Webster played a role in the formation of an American form of the English language with his publication of the \ldblquote Blue Back\rdblquote  speller, which was formerly titled The American Spelling Book in 1786. This would quite probably be the spelling book that Miss Watson turns to in her attempt to educate Huck and draw him into the sphere of conventionality. Webster's Speller not only attempted to distinguish American language from British, but also sought to introduce homogeneity into the language of a large and diverse country. As Mark Twain's Explanatory indicates, homogeneity never really caught on. Webster also published the first dictionary in the United States in 1806, the first of many iterations that were guided by the same objectives as spelling book: American distinction and homogeneity. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Calls for a distinctive American literature and in a similar way for American expressions in the visual arts grew out of complaints that American writers were simply imitating themes, forms, and styles of British writers. In order to create a distinctive national identity, one that would bind the young nation together, American critics urged writers to create a literature that addressed the values and virtues of the United States. The work of James Fenimore Cooper and his The Leatherstocking Tales and certainly, Nathaniel Hawthorne's The Scarlet Letter were, you know, were attempts to do just that. \par
\par
 \par
\par
By any measure, Adventures of Huckleberry Huckleberry Finn succeeds in doing both. First, by addressing the American themes of race and the natural landscape. And second, by representing the vitality of American forms of language influenced by region, social class, and racial identity. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now one form of writing that projected a distinctly American character was the genre of American humor called Old SouthWest Humor, which was explicitly dialect writing. It was often written by educated easterners such as James Russell Lowell or Matthew Whittier among others, who used idiosyncratic spelling to represent the unlettered homespun speech of people who lived in the hinterlands. Now Mark Twain's early writing was in this vein. We see it in the story that earned him his first national attention, \ldblquote The Celebrated Jumping Frog of Calaveras County.\rdblquote  This story, like a lot of old SouthWest humor, takes the form of a frame tale. A frame tale begins with one narrator who then describes a scene in which another narrator takes over. And then the original narrator re-enters at the end to offer a closing commentary. \par
\par
 \par
\par
In SouthWest Humor, as in the jumping frog story, the first narrator is often a speaker from the East, one who speaks standard English and the internal narrator is a dialect narrator, often called a vernacular narrator. The interaction of these two different registers of language amount to a verbal competition, a contest between the frame narrator who represents the center of the society and the vernacular speaker who represents the margins of that society. In these cases, the linguistic differences are symbols of a power differential in the culture and SouthWest Humor often delighted in having the vernacular speaker come out as the winner in the competition. \par
\par
 \par
\par
In Roughing It, Mark Twain's memoir of his travels to the Far West, he includes a number of scenes in which awkward communication between speakers of different dialects yield conflicts that are both humorous and meaningful. In The adventures of Tom Sawyer, we see a related but slightly different conflict. The third person narrator's voice is mature and genteel, which establishes distance between him and the boys in this Mississippi River town. Their mischievous pranks and superstitions and especially their language provide the readers chief amusement. \par
\par
 \par
\par
In Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, Twain takes a different tack. He dispenses with the avuncular third person narrator and instead has Huck Finn be the teller of his own story. Huck first appeared in The Adventures of Tom Sawyer as an outsider in the town whom other boys found intriguing and about whom mothers feared their sons might follow down a wayward path. Now Twain's first idea after completing the Tom Sawyer novel was to have Tom go on and tell his own story that would take him into adulthood, no doubt inspired by books like Charles Dickens' Great Expectations. But he quickly recognized that Tom wouldn't do. He called him a one-horse character, a figure with a limited bag of tricks. \par
\par
 \par
\par
So, he decided instead to follow-up the success of the first novel by giving the narrative over to Huck Finn. \par
\par
 \par
\par
