Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: The Language of Huck \par
\par
Prof Larry Howe \par
\par
Roosevelt University \par
\par
 \par
\par
Adventures of Huckleberry Finn begins rather memorably with Huck addressing the reader like this: \par
\par
 \par
\par
You don\rquote t know about me without you have read a book by the name of \lquote The Adventures of Tom Sawyer\rquote , but that ain\rquote t no matter. That book was made by Mr. Mark Twain, and he told the truth, mainly. There was things which he stretched, but mainly he told the truth. That is nothing. I never seen anybody but lied one time or another, without it was Aunt Polly, or the widow, or maybe Mary. Aunt Polly\emdash Tom\rquote s Aunt Polly, she is\emdash and Mary, and the Widow Douglas is all told about in that book, which is mostly a true book, with some stretchers, as I said before.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (12) \par
\par
 \par
\par
This is an interesting way to start off this new novel for a few reasons. First, Huck's language is clearly not standard. The reader recognizes that phrases like \ldblquote that ain't no matter\rdblquote  or \ldblquote there was things which he stretched\rdblquote  identify the speaker as uneducated. So, we're in unconventional territory from the start. Second, Huck acknowledges his earlier appearance in another book. A book that he has apparently read and also assumes the reader of his book may also be aware. Third, he says that Mark Twain told the truth mainly. And he undercuts Twain's reliability by repeatedly reminding us that he \ldblquote told some stretchers.\rdblquote  And while he dismisses this early on as \ldblquote no matter,\rdblquote  the repetition suggests that it is a matter of some importance. By calling attention to Twain's troubled credibility, Huck implies that his story will be free of the misimpressions or stretchers that Mark Twain was guilty of in the first book. \par
\par
 \par
\par
This opening offers readers an amusing idea that a fictional character can separate himself from the authority of the writer who created him and live autonomously, which implicitly obscures the fictionality of the story that Huck is about to narrate to us. Huck seems to assure us that in telling his own tale, he'll set the record straight without stretchers in a in an authentic voice not conditioned by artfulness. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now in setting the scene in the first chapter, Huck tells us about the annoying rigors of living with The Widow Douglas, where one must \ldblquote grumble\rdblquote  over one's food before eating. Even her reading of Biblical stories, which initially enthralled him, leave him cold when he learns that the people in them, as he says, \ldblquote had been dead a considerable long time; so then I didn't care no more about him, because I don't take no stock in dead people.\rdblquote  (13) \par
\par
 \par
\par
He tells us that he has a similar scepticism about the insistence of Miss Watson, the widow's sister, on the who insists on the power of prayer: \par
\par
 \par
\par
She told me to pray every day, and whatever I asked for, I would get it. But it warn't so. I tried it. Once I got a fish-line but no hooks. It warn't no good to me without hooks. I tried for the hooks three or four times, but somehow I couldn't make it work. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (23) \par
\par
 \par
\par
 \par
\par
While many readers find Huck's outlook and especially his colorful turns of phrase amusing and even appealing, others saw these as reasons to criticize the book. Soon after its publication, the Concord Public Library in Concord, Massachusetts, home to literary luminaries like Ralph Waldo Emerson and Louisa May Alcott, and thus the center of polite society, condemned adventures Huckleberry Finn as \ldblquote the veriest trash\rdblquote  and warned that making a hero out of a truant with no regard for religion or a sense of morality could corrupt children who read the book. The critique is based in part on morality, but also on the rough-hewn language in which Huck speaks. But by taking offense at his language, critics of the book ignore the growth that Huck undergoes across the narrative. He begins the story as a boy raised in a culture that accepted slavery readily and therefore saw black people as something less than human. But by the end, he has gained a respect for Jim that has come through his experiences with him and also from Jim's own account of himself. Critics who reject Huck because of his marginal status failed to see that his marginality allows him to form a relationship with an even more marginalized figure like Jim. Huck tells Tom Sawyer in confidence that he has even eaten with black people, a taboo in the culture's rigid segregation. When Huck fears that Pap is back in town, he goes to Jim and appeals for his occult knowledge. And when the two runaways meet up on Jackson's Island, they readily form an alliance, one reinforced by Huck's exclamation, \ldblquote They're after us!\rdblquote  when he returns from his visit to the outskirts of town. Because both Huck and Jim are escaping from oppressive forces, Huck finds himself in situations that require him to use his wits. \par
\par
 \par
\par
His meeting with Judith Loftus gives us insight into Huck's ability to worm his way out of tight situations. Readers who listen to Huck without discounting him for his vernacular recognize that he is quick on his feet when cornered. Huck's encounters require him to improvise different identities that will protect him and Jim from being caught. Moreover, those who condemn the book for immorality ignore the fact that in many of Huck's encounters, he acts ethically. For example, he knows that the outlaws on the sinking steamboat, The Walter Scott, are in jeopardy for their lives, and he acts to have them rescued. Even though he has a pang of conscience about helping Jim, he lures the slave catchers into drawing conclusions that keep Jim safe. He has an unspeakable reaction to having witnessed the slaughter in the Grangerford Shepherdson feud. He sees the king and the duke for the con men that they are, and his sympathy for the Wilkes girls leads him to expose them as frauds. And his horror to learn that the two frauds have sold Jim into captivity lead him to examine his commitment to either the law of slave property or his loyalty to Jim.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Some critics have seen the last ten chapters as a failure on Twain's part. They see Huck being mistaken as Tom Sawyer, who arrives in this on the scene shortly thereafter as a sign that Twain had run out of ideas. Still, many more scholars today broadly agree that Tom's elaborate ruse to free Jim as a means of maintaining his captivity for Tom's pleasure functions as a symbol of the failure of reconstruction. The nation's failure to enforce the rights of former slaves after The Civil War led to a de facto enslavement by other means. Critics who condemn Huck similarly for not following through on his vow to free Jim and see him as a willing pawn of Tom Sawyer, overlook an important distinction between Huck and Tom. This distinction comes into focus when we compare two parallel moments in which the boys appear to act similarly but actually proceed on very different motives. In the first instance, this is when Judith Loftus reveals to Huck that the community has assumed that Jim has been murdered and Huck reports that he blurted: \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote Why he\endash\ldblquote  \par
\par
I stopped. I reckoned I better keep still. She run on, and never noticed I put in at all. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (74) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now compare that moment to the moment when Huck and Jim and Tom Sawyer are reunited at Phelps Farm. Huck tells Tom of his plan to steal Jim out of captivity. And Tom says: \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote What! Why Jim is\endash\ldblquote  \par
\par
He stopped and went to studying. I says:  \par
\par
\ldblquote I know what you'll say. You'll say it's a dirty low-down business; but what if it is?\endash I'm low down; and I'm agoing to steal him, and I want you to keep mum and not let on. Will you?\rdblquote  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (74) \par
\par
 \par
\par
 \par
\par
In both scenes, the speaker is about to respond but checks himself. Despite the similarity of abrupted speech, their reasons for going silent could not be more different. Huck chucks his speech so that Jim will remain free, whereas Tom stops himself in order to keep Huck from learning that Jim is already free, a secret that Tom maintains until the novel's final scene. And with the secret undisclosed at this pivotal point in the novel, Tom can convert Huck's plan into a scheme to perpetuate Jim's captivity. Now there's also further significance embedded in the phrase that Huck uses to enlist Tom's support. Huck says, \ldblquote I want you to keep mum and not let on.\rdblquote  Now the verb \ldblquote let on\rdblquote  is kind of a colloquialism is one of those unusual words that has two opposite meanings. It can mean what Huck means here, to speak out, to reveal, as when he remarks about hearing Jim mourning the loss of his family, Huck says: \par
\par
 \par
\par
I didn't take notice, nor let on. I knowed what it was about. He was thinking about his wife and children, away up yonder, and he was low and homesick. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (192) \par
\par
 \par
\par
But for Tom, the word usually means to pretend, to conceal the truth. We see this second meaning in operation when Tom decides to use a pick instead of a case knife to dig Jim out of the cabin where he's being held. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Tom agrees to \ldblquote let on\rdblquote  that the pick is a case knife, a much less efficient tool for digging and in this way, Tom can pretend that he's following the rules of adventure stories that dictate his actions. Huck does use the verb \ldblquote to let on\rdblquote  to mean pretend in one important instance, during his moment of crisis upon learning about Jim's captivity. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Having written a note to Miss Watson about Jim's whereabouts, he is on the verge of a conversion experience. But when he attempts to pray for forgiveness and the strength to reform, he comes up short. He realizes that he can't be forgiven. He says: \par
\par
 \par
\par
It was because my heart warn\rquote t right; it's because I warn\rquote t square; it was because I was playing double. I was letting on to give up my sin, but away inside of me I was holding on to the biggest one of all. I was trying to make my mouth say I would do the right thing and the clean thing [\'85] but deep down in me I knowed it was a lie [\'85] I found that out. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (252) \par
\par
 \par
\par
 \par
\par
Here, Huck recognizes that pretending and simply saying something, the kind of posturing that Tom readily indulges, is morally wrong and it's especially wrong because of its impact on Jim. Huck projects his ethics and his integrity in his honest language. \par
\par
 \par
\par
{{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://massolit.io/courses/twain-huckleberry-finn-howe/the-language-of-huck }}{\fldrslt{https://massolit.io/courses/twain-huckleberry-finn-howe/the-language-of-huck\ul0\cf0}}}}\f1\fs22   \par
