\par
Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: The Language of Jim \par
\par
Prof Larry Howe \par
\par
Roosevelt University \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain's representation of Jim's dialect, the Negro dialect that he mentions first in the Explanatory, is a point of considerable debate among scholars. For some critics, Jim's speech is an example of minstrelsy, a degrading form of racist mimicry that began on the American stage in the nineteenth century. Minstrel shows had white performers in blackface often ridiculing blacks as buffoons for the entertainment of white audiences. Those critics point to Twain's own words of appreciation for minstrel theatre. But this evidence is mitigated by research done by Sharon McCoy, who has shown that Twain's favourite minstrel troupe, The San Francisco Minstrels, projected themselves very differently from run of the mill companies. The San Francisco Minstrels satire was focused not on blacks, but on contemporary corruption and financial injustices. The lead actor was adept at imitating leading political and social figures while in his blackface persona. So he was demeaning those no notable white leaders, not the blacks that his makeup and costume represented. Twain attended their performances in San Francisco and later in New York, and also wrote approvingly of their sophisticated satire. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Scholars who credit Mark Twain's representation of Jim's language point to Twain's first writing in black vernacular, \lquote A True Story, Repeated Word for word As I Heard It,\rquote  which was published in The Atlantic monthly magazine in 1874. This piece is important for at least three reasons: First, it was Twain's first appearance in The Atlantic, the most prestigious literary magazine of the day, where many America's notable writers appeared. So being admitted into this company launched his career to a new level. Second, it was also his first story by his own admission that has, \ldblquote No humor in it.\rdblquote  So it signals his desire to be taken seriously. Third, most importantly, \lquote A True Story\rquote  relates an emotional story of a formerly enslaved woman's suffering, resilience, and reward, and in her own voice. It begins as a frame tale, which I described in the first segment of these lectures. Twain, as the frame narrator, begins by describing a scene on a summer night on the porch of his sister in law's home. He notes the jovial laughter of the family's cook, aunt Rachel, who sits below them because she was their servant and as he says, \ldblquote colored.\rdblquote  Observing aunt Rachel's happy personality, he suggests to her that it reflects her life free of care and worry. She asks him with a doubtful tone:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote Misto C\emdash , is you in \lquote arnest?\rdblquote  \par
\par
 \par
\par
And then proceeds to tell her story of having been enslaved, of the agony of being separated from her husband and children in a slave auction. Her story moves ahead then twenty years during The Civil War. The plantation where she was enslaved was liberated by a black regiment of the Union Army. Among the soldiers is her youngest son, and the story closes with their heartfelt reunion. Here, aunt Rachel's authoritative narration disrupts the conventional form of the frame tale. The power of her story makes it impossible for Mr C to provide a closing comment. She has the last word. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now the model for Aunt Rachel was Mary Ann Cord, the cook for Twain's sister-in-law at her home, Quarry Farm in Elmira, New York. Cord\rquote s life story matches the one that Aunt Rachel narrates. Now Twain and his family spent their summers at Quarry Farm for twenty years and became closely acquainted with all of the people in the household. And on those summer evenings, the entire household gathered on the front porch of the hilltop home to discuss topics of the day. The occasion for Cord's storytelling was one of those evenings. Much as Twain says in the Explanatory of Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, his representation of Aunt Rachel's dialect was not in any way haphazard. In fact, he spent a lot of time working on adapting her voice to the page. Reading it aloud over and over until he was sure that his literary record of it was true, as the title explicitly says, to the power of her speech. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now after studying Mary Ann Cord's voice and that of John Lewis, a black tenant farmer in Elmira, Twain gained insight into writing Jim's speech. We might also note that Jim's dialogue with Huck evolves across the narrative. And it's worth paying attention not only to how Jim speaks, but to what he says. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Early on, it would seem that some of the charges of minstrelsy have some merit. But we should keep in mind that Huck is telling the story, including conveying the dialogue. And his perspective on Jim would reflect the white bias of the community. Similarly, early in their meeting, Jim's deference to Huck should be viewed as a carefully calibrated response to a boy who could just as easily turn Jim in as aid in his flight. The fact that Jim has withheld the knowledge that Huck's father, Pap, is dead until the very end of the story is evidence that Jim is being strategic and keeping Huck committed to their journey. A similar condition affects Jim's behaviour in the final chapters, where he must follow Tom's ridiculous instructions in order to placate him and prevent him from either losing interest in Jim's escape or worse. In between these early and late scenes, Jim speaks with a voice that is both dignified and self-reflective. We'll look at two examples. In the first, Huck and Jim have been separated in the disorienting fog. When Huck returns to the raft, he finds Jim asleep. Jim awakens and is relieved that Huck is safe. Rather than accept Jim's affection, Huck convinces Jim that they'd never been separated and that Jim had only dreamed that they were. And he encourages Jim to interpret the dream. After Jim gives a lengthy reading of the significance of his supposed dream, Huck points to the debris on the raft that gives material evidence of what had really happened and mockingly asks Jim what the debris symbolizes. Jim takes his time and then defiantly puts Huck's in his place: \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote What do dey stan\rquote  for? I\rquote se gwyne to tell you. When I got all wore out wid work, en wid de callin\rquote  for you, en went to sleep, my heart wuz mos\rquote  broke bekase you wuz los\rquote , en I didn' k\rquote yer no\rquote  mo\rquote  what become er me en de\rquote  raf\rquote . En when I wake up en fin\rquote  you back agin, all safe an\rquote  soun\rquote , de tears come, en I got I could a got down on my knees en kiss yo\rquote  foot, I\rquote s thankful. En all you wuz thinkin\rquote  \lquote bout wuz how you could make a fool uv ole Jim wid a lie. Dat truck dah is trash; en trash is what people is dat puts dirt on de head er dey fren\rquote s en makes \lquote em ashamed. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (105) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now Jim then retires to the tent on the raft, leaving Huck to ponder his humiliation. Fifteen minutes later, he apologizes to Jim. And he says:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
I didn't do him no more mean tricks, and I wouldn't done that one if I'd known it would make him feel that way. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (105) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now Jim's direct plain-spoken condemnation of Huck\rquote s trick calls out his cruelty and shows his dignity as one who deserves respect. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now the second example of Jim's narration, of have of his having struck his daughter when enraged by mistaking her deafness as disobedience gives us a slightly different insight into Jim's character. In this quiet moment on the river, Jim's dialect speech offers meaningful ambiguity, especially with the respect to one important word, recalling his confrontation with Elizabeth. Jim says:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
But this time I somehow got to talking to him about his wife and young ones; and by-and-by he says: \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote What makes me feel so bad dis time \rquote uz bekase I hear sumpn over yonder on de bank like a whack, er a slam, while ago, en it mine me er de time I treat my little \rquote Lizabeth so ornery. She warn\rquote t on\rquote y \rquote bout fo\rquote  year ole, en she tuck de sk\rquote yarlet fever, en had a powful rough spell; but she got well, en one day she was a-stannin\rquote  aroun\rquote , en I says to her, I says: \par
\par
\ldblquote\lquote Shet de do\rquote .\rquote  \par
\par
\ldblquote She never done it; jis\rquote  stood dah, kiner smilin\rquote  up at me. It make me mad; en I says agin, mighty loud, I says: \par
\par
\ldblquote\lquote Doan\rquote  you hear me?\emdash shet de do\rquote !\rquote  \par
\par
\ldblquote She jis stood de same way, kiner smilin\rquote  up. I was a-bilin\rquote ! I says: \par
\par
\ldblquote\lquote I lay I make you mine!\rquote  \par
\par
\ldblquote En wid dat I fetch\rquote  her a slap side de head dat sont her a-sprawlin\rquote . \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (192 - 193) \par
\par
 \par
\par
And Jim goes on to tell how he discovered that Elizabeth was deaf rather than disobedient, and he laments his violent outburstut we'll stop here to highlight the words Jim uses before striking his daughter. He says, \ldblquote I lay, I make you mine!\rdblquote  Now in Standard English, one way to translate this is, \ldblquote I swear I'll make you mind,\rdblquote  as in \ldblquote Mind your elders / I'll make you obey me.\rdblquote  In fact, the manuscript uses that word mind with a d. Now we don't know when or why Twain changed the word to \ldblquote mine.\rdblquote  And it may seem simply to have been to capture the sound of black speech with its propensity for dropping final consonants. But even if Twain crafted this shift because it sounds authentic, it's nonetheless true that Jim's dialect lingers in a space between asserting his authority to make her obey, to mind him, and asserting his ownership over her in making her mine, much like a slave master asserts ownership through physical force. We see this assertion of ownership most notably in the literary archetype of a brutal slave holder, Simon Legree, the villain of Harriet Beecher Stowe's Uncle Tom's Cabin. Frustrated by Uncle Tom's religious devotion, Legree insists that Uncle Tom admit that he is merely property. Legree says:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Here, you rascal, you make believe to be so pious,\endash  didn't you never hear, out of yer Bible, \lquote Servants, obey your masters? An\rquote t I yer master? Didn't I pay down twelve hundred dollars, cash, for all there is inside your old cussed black shell? An\rquote t yer mine, now, body and soul?\rdblquote  he said, giving Tom a violent kick with his heavy boot, \ldblquote tell me!\rdblquote  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Harriet Beecher Stowe, Uncle Tom\rquote s Cabin, Chapter 33 \par
\par
 \par
\par
Now Jim's use of \ldblquote mine\rdblquote  is a by-product of his having been raised in slavery, a dehumanizing system that employs a perverse language of ownership. The paternalistic foundations of the slavery system instruct the kind of obedience by force that Jim expected from Elizabeth. But in Jim's moment of recollection, he acknowledges the error of his claim to own her. He has come to understand that his bond with Elizabeth is not a property right, but an emotional connection that entails the responsibility to love and protect her. Huck reflects on Jim's paternal affection, noting: \par
\par
 \par
\par
I do believe he cared just as much as much for his people as white folks does for their\rquote n. It don't seem natural, but I reckon it's so. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Mark Twain, The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (192) \par
\par
 \par
\par
This is an ironic remark coming from Huck because Jim stands in sharp contrast to Huck's father, Pap, who only comes to town early in the story because he's heard that Huck is now rich. And he intends to claim that money because it, like Huck, is, in his mind, his property. In distinguishing Papp's degraded form of fatherhood and Jim's honorable one, we can measure the merit of Jim, a merit that those who discount him for his dialect speech overlook. \par
\par
 \par
\par
